\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 "Everything is too expensive": this is the year in review\b0\par\par\uc2 1,526 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 Dezember 2022\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 Chances are that every time you went to the supermarket this year you noticed that prices had gone up, you may not have been able to afford it and had to reduce the size of your purchases, or you may have had to increase your food budget. But the cost of living didn't stop there, the same thing happened if you were planning to order a house for the weekend, pay your energy bill, go on a trip or buy a "premiere" this December. "Everything is too expensive" was the most frequently heard phrase throughout 2022.\par \par \uc2 According to the National Administrative Department of Statistics (DANE), the cost of living has increased 12.53% in the last year (from November 2021 to November 2022), and the worst blow to the pocket has been felt by the poorest families for whom inflation has been 14.34%.\par \par \uc2 Why the difference? Because the price of food has risen the most (27.08%) and the poorest families spend a greater proportion of their income on food. For example, according to the DANE, the price of cassava for home consumption increased 107.64% in one year, the price of arracacha, yams and other tubers increased 104.09% and Colombians paid 89.63% more for onions.\par \par \uc2 Why did prices increase? The first thing to say is that inflation is not a phenomenon that is only experienced in Colombia; the whole world has been facing high prices for reasons that are very difficult to control.\par \par \uc2 The first explanation is that since 2021, with the reactivation of the global economy after the confinements to stop the advance of the pandemic, demand soared, families around the world went out to buy without control, very good news for trade that had experienced paralysis, but supply failed to keep pace with demand, companies could not produce as fast and we must not forget that there was a crisis of containers that were not enough to move global trade. This started to make products more expensive.\par \par \uc2 In addition to all this, in February the Russian invasion of Ukraine exploded, and beyond the regrettable results in human lives that the war has meant, it put the world economy on the ropes. Much oil and gas -Russia alone produces some 11 million barrels of oil per day-, cereals, strategic minerals for the manufacture of technology and automobiles, and fertilizers -42% of the fertilizers consumed by Colombia before the invasion came from those two countries- come out of that region.\par \par \uc2 This war not only made it more expensive to produce in Colombia, but also in the rest of the world. Europe depends to a large extent on Russian gas supplies, so its energy costs rose and the price of its production went through the roof.\par \par \uc2 And locally, the prolonged winter that damaged many crops weighed heavily in the increase in prices; in fact, coffee growers reduced their production by close to one million bags this year, even in the midst of the best prices in history.\par \par \uc2 Solution in sight The hope is that the increase in the interest rate of the Bank of the Republic will begin to reduce consumption and in this way prices will begin to be controlled. At least this is the hope of Finance Minister Jos\u233\'00\'E9 Antonio Ocampo, who told EL COLOMBIANO that it is natural that inflation in Colombia will follow the same course as that of the United States, which has been falling for three months as a result of the tightening of monetary policy.\par \par \uc2 The most expensive dollar in history\par \par \uc2 The Colombian economy faced for the first time what seemed impossible: a dollar at $5,000. During the pandemic, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  had reached a record high of $4,153 (on March 23) amid the uncertainty caused by the confinements, and from there it slowly dropped.\par \par \uc2 So, since the beginning of this year, the dollar began to climb, approaching -and at times surpassing- $4,000, but in the second half of the year, driven mainly by the increase in interest rates in the United States, which promote the outflow of investment capital to that country, and the election of the first leftist president in Colombia, the devaluation of the peso deepened.\par \par \uc2 On June 2, the dollar surpassed the historical record and reached $4,198.77, and on November 3 it surpassed $5,000 for the first time, as the market's representative rate for that day was $5,015.84. The maximum exchange rate was recorded in the following week when it reached $5,061.21 (November 5).\par \par \uc2 Against all odds, the dollar began to fall, ending the year slightly above $4,700. All think tanks assure that this is the new reality of the national economy, since the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will not be below $4,000 again, and here the necessary adjustments will have to be made so that the expensive dollar is as less traumatic as possible.\par \par \uc2 Everyone loses and some win With an expensive dollar, imports, which represent a large part of domestic consumption, especially in technology, vehicles, auto parts and clothing, have become more expensive.\par \par \uc2 On the other hand, the Colombian economy depends on imported fertilizers, almost 80% comes from abroad, so local food production also became more expensive, so the dollar also pushed up the already high inflation.\par \par \uc2 2022 was a particularly difficult year for Venezuelans sending remittances to their country from Colombia, because the pesos did not yield when converted into dollars, while Colombians abroad made a party sending resources to the country.\par \par \uc2 Exporters also took a good cut, as commodity prices were high and the dollar gave them an even bigger boost.\par \par \uc2 Rising interest rates\par \par \uc2 The rise in the cost of living around the world forced central banks to increase their interest rates. The global economy was coming off a period of at least a couple of years of near-zero rates to promote economic growth during and after the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 The United States led the way throughout the year, the other issuers, including the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , watched their movements carefully because they indicated two things: the speed with which rates were transmitted to the reduction of consumption and therefore the decrease in inflation, and the appetite of investors for that economy with which we must compete.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 United States Federal Reserve\uc2  (FED), led by \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2 , had a rate between 0% and 0.25% and is now at 4.5%, which is considered very high for a power. The \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  set its rates a couple of weeks ago at a maximum of 2.75%, and all countries have been increasing it over the course of 2022. \b \highlight22\uc2 Colombia\b0 \highlight\uc2  already has an \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate of 12%, the highest in the last 23 years.\par \par \uc2 This has led to a reduction in access to credit, especially mortgages, whose interest rate has gone from 8% to 18%.\par \par \uc2 Both Leonardo Villar, manager of Banco de la Rep\u250\'00\'FAblica, and Jos\u233\'00\'E9 Antonio Ocampo, Minister of Finance, are confident that this \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate, together with the reduction of rainfall, will lead to a break in inflation in the coming months and that it will begin to converge towards the 3% target. The official forecast is that in 2023 inflation will be 7%, but there are so many factors that influence that you never know.\par \par \uc2 And on the business side, 2022 was also a surprise: the castling is wobbling with the PTBs.\par \par \uc2 After six takeover bids, Jaime Gilinski, through his companies Nugil and JGDB Holding, obtained 38% of the shares of Grupo Sura and 31% of those of \uc2 Grupo Nutresa\uc2 , consolidating his position as one of the main shareholders of the so-called \uc2 Grupo Empresarial Antioque\u241\'00\'F1o (GEA)\uc2 , in what has been catalogued as a hostile takeover.\par \par \uc2 He was unsuccessful in the takeover bid he launched for \uc2 Grupo Argos\uc2 , which was declared deserted because it did not reach the minimum required by the tycoon, so he did not achieve any participation in the infrastructure holding company.\par \par \uc2 Beyond the participation achieved so far by the banker from Cali, what was seen throughout the year is a dispute for the control of the companies in the country, both in the shareholders' meetings and in the boards of directors as well as in the courts. Requests from both sides to the superintendencies of Health, Industry and Commerce and Finance and before the judges of the Republic have been the order of the day. It is not surprising that there will be long litigations to solve the controversies, remembering the 10 years of litigations that the businessmen from Paisas and Gilinski had when the latter sold Banco de Colombia to them.\par \par \uc2 According to several market analysts, it would not be strange either, that new takeover bids with better prices are presented either by Gilinski or by his Arab partner IHC, which has a greater financial muscle.\par \par \uc2 It should not be forgotten that Gilinski's target is \uc2 Bancolombia\uc2 , since he wants to merge it with his bank GNB Sudameris, and it seems that he has not yet run out of patience in this obsession.\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020221231eicv003nq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
