\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 Colombian peso plunges to historic low\b0\par\par\uc2 354 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 November 2022\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 The Colombian peso is making history, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  crossed the 5,000 units per dollar barrier for the first time, in the face of local uncertainty derived from the current government's economic policies and after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  decided not to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the exchange market. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fell 1.36% and reached a new historic low of 5,005 units per dollar in a session with high volatility and marked by international caution while awaiting the decisions of the \uc2 US Federal Reserve\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Why does the Colombian peso fall? So far this year, the Colombian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has depreciated 18%, while in October the depreciation was 7%, in both cases it is one of the largest falls among Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The Colombian peso has accelerated its weakening against the dollar since June, after \uc2 Gustavo Petro\uc2  was elected president of the country. According to a \uc2 Bloomberg\uc2  note, investors are concerned about policies such as his pledge to begin phasing out fossil fuels, which account for about half of the country's exports, criticism of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  interest rate hikes and his intention to implement a tax on capital outflows. Given the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's performance, there was speculation that \b \highlight22\uc2 Colombia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  might \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to halt the decline; however, the bank decided not to take such action. In addition, brokers highlighted the adverse message of the appointment of Carlos Gustavo Cano as head of the board of directors of the Colombian oil company \uc2 Ecopetrol\uc2 , who in less than 24 hours was replaced by Sa\u250\'00\'FAl Kattan, after Petro opposed the decision, according to Reuters, which cites two sources with knowledge of the talks. The Colombian state owns 88.5% of \uc2 Ecopetrol\uc2 , the country's largest company. "The decision sends the wrong message and challenges the independence of the board, which may justify investor risk aversion that has translated into implied equity risk for \uc2 Ecopetrol\uc2 ," \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  said in a note to clients. Citogroup also said Kattan was elected as head of the board in a split decision. With information from Reuters.\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020221101eib1009pt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
