\par\par \b \uc2 Colombia  \highlight22\uc2 central bank \highlight\uc2  board chief defends  \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange \highlight\uc2  policy\b0\par\par\uc2 352 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Juli 2022\par 18:32\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 BOGOTA, July 21 (Reuters) - The board chief of \uc2 Colombia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Thursday defended the monetary policy authority's decision not to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in movements of the country's peso, even as market volatility pushed other central banks in the region to take action.\par \par \uc2 Colombia's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fell 2.3% to 4,424 pesos to the dollar early in the session, barely a week since it hit record lows against the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as fears of recession in the United States and Europe boosted the dollar's attractiveness as a refuge asset.\par \par \uc2 "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's policy is to leave the exchange rate float freely, because when there is free-float it carries the adjustment mechanism with it," board chief Leonardo Villar said in a virtual meeting organized by investment manager Skandia.\par \par \uc2 "The important thing for the future is the perspective that things can go up or down and that the market itself is in charge of adjusting them," he said, adding the banks position would discourage capital flows moving in just one direction.\par \par \uc2 Last week, \uc2 Chile's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  agreed to a $25 billion \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market due to the recent advance of the US dollar.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Colombia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  last \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in 2020 during the early stages of the coronavirus pandemic, when it adopted measures to boost liquidity in dollars.\par \par \uc2 Although most analysts rule out the possibility that \uc2 Colombia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would adopt measures to contain the peso's volatility, some argue it is time that the monetary policy authority make its presence felt amid broad turbulence in one way or another.\par \par \uc2 "We don't foresee \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Julio Cesar Romero, chief economist at investment holding company Corficolombiana, said.\par \par \uc2 "However, we believe that now is the time for an \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to reinforce the liquidity in dollars in the system and help mitigate the volatility of the peso against the dollar," he added. (Reporting by Nelson Bocanegra Writing by Oliver Griffin Editing by Alistair Bell) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2022-7-21T17:32:49.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020220721ei7l03a21\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
