\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 Economic slowdown could impact employment generation in Latin America\b0\par\par\uc2 899 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Juli 2022\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 After the peak of the pandemic caused by covid-19 and the strongest restrictions that set back the global economy, it seems to stabilize and return to 2019 levels; however, the slowdown of this dynamic and the possible negative numbers would impact one of the most relevant factors for Latin America: employment.The main economies of the region have already returned or are close to the unemployment levels that were registered at the end of 2019, months before the impact of the confinements. The Nation that today registers the lowest rate is Mexico, whose record reaches 3.56%, followed by Peru, with 6.8% after touching a maximum 16.5% at the end of 2020.In the same order also appears Argentina with 7% of job shortage in the midst of a macroeconomic panorama that seems to worsen with an inflation above in 64%. In addition, Chile follows with 7.8%, Colombia with 10.65% and Brazil with 11.1%.In the short and medium term, the situation could become precarious.\par \par \uc2  According to Julio Romero, chief economist of Corficolombiana, during the last two years we have seen a recovery of jobs, a fact that has evidenced the good dynamics of Colombia and the region.Read here: Will the dollar fall again below $4,000? Projections on what is to come for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 "However, this data has not moved at the same pace as production. What the Economic Tracking Index (ISE) showed us in May is that activity is close to 9% above pre-pandemic levels, which means that we are producing more with the same level as then," said the expert.In addition to this, there is a structural problem that marks a public policy challenge in terms of how to coordinate the public and private sectors to attack the lack of jobs."In the event of a global recession, this will be felt in a lower demand for export products, a drop in remittances and investment amounts, as well as higher exchange rates. The most vulnerable will be low-income citizens and those linked to the informal economy," he concluded.In fact, according to the \uc2 International Labor Organization (ILO)\uc2 , in Latin America and the Caribbean there have been important advances in the legal coverage of people who perform domestic work, but the high level of informality in this labor sector and the difficulties in enforcing the laws leave most of them without any type of social protection."The region faces a very clear challenge, which is the effective enforcement of laws on domestic work, so that the progress achieved in recent years is reflected in people's quality of life," said ILO Regional Director for Latin America and the Caribbean, Claudia Coenjaerts.\par \par \uc2 The role of interest rates and inflation Central banks and the prices of products consumed on a daily basis have become the protagonists of the global landscape. Today, issuers are seeking to halt an excessive rise in the Consumer Price Index (CPI), which has made goods and services more expensive, but which in turn has led the United States and the \uc2 European Union\uc2  to talk about the arrival of a recession that would hit the economy even harder.In Latin America, the outlook is not very different from what is being projected by the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  and the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in terms of monetary policy. While the Fed has raised its rate by 150 basis points to 1.75% so far this year and in Europe it remains at 0%, the main economies in the region have decided to tighten their \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by an average of 400 bp over the first seven months of the year.Among the strong economies in the region, the nation that has seen the largest increase is Chile, with a 575 bp rise, from 4% at the beginning of the year to 9.75% at the beginning of July. This is followed by Colombia, with an increase of 450 bps, from 3% to 7.5%; Brazil, with 400 bps, from 9.25% to 13.25%; Peru, with 335 bps, from 2.5% to 6% and Mexico, with 225 bps, from 5.5% to 7.75%.According to Sergio Olarte, chief economist at \uc2 Scotiabank Colpatria\uc2 , each country has its idiosyncrasies. "I am not sure that \b \highlight22\uc2 Colombia\b0 \highlight\uc2  has taken much longer to change the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate than other strong economies in the region. \b \highlight22\uc2 Colombia\b0 \highlight\uc2  lowered this benchmark figure much less, while Peru and Chile downgraded it almost to zero, we brought it up to 1.75%, so we didn't have to start so fast."This picture contrasts with the behavior of annualized inflation in these countries. Leading the list is Chile, with a cost of living growth of 869 bps to 12.49%, followed by Colombia, whose indicator has grown 604 bps in the last twelve months, reaching 9.67%, the highest record in 22 years.This country is joined by Peru, with a 556 bp increase to 8.81%; Brazil, with a 354 bp rebound to 11.89% and Mexico, with a 211 bp increase to 7.99%. "Colombia has a little more time left to raise interest rates; however, in general the markets are seeing that Latin America was the first to start raising them, which sends a good signal with world peers," added Olarte.\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020220720ei7k00ej8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
