\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 Latin American  \highlight22\uc2 currencies \highlight\uc2  are no longer on the brink of collapse\b0\par\par\uc2 283 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Dezember 2019\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 We can say it in a very low voice, but the worst may have already happened for Latin America's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Brazil, Chile and Colombia are reducing losses in December after falling to historic lows last month, following a wave of violent protests that threatened to depress already slow growth in the region.\par \par \uc2 Chile's weight rose 3.3%, Colombia's weight rose 1.8% and Brazil's real gained 0.5% in the first four days of the month. It may not seem like much, but it's Chile's longest winning streak since June and \b \highlight22\uc2 Colombia\b0 \highlight\uc2  since September. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Chile and Brazil has played an important role, while Colombia is relieved as the protests did not get out of hand as many feared.\par \par \uc2 Of course, not all \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  have left their problems behind. The Argentine peso remains close to an all-time low amid strict monetary controls as Alberto Fernandez prepares to take over the presidency on December 10.\par \par \uc2 "There is a possibility that Latin America's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market will continue to rebound to close this gap" between the rest of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of developing countries, which surpassed the region in November, said Ilya Gofshteyn, senior macro market strategist Standard Chartered Bank in New York. "Lower liquidity at the end of the year creates unfavourable seasonal dynamics, but I would expect the BRL, MXN, COP and CLP \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to start in 2020 with a stronger tone."\par \par \uc2 The entry \b \highlight22\uc2 Currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Latin America are no longer on the brink of collapse was first published in La Tercera.\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020191205efc5004kw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
