\par\par \b \uc2 EMERGING MARKETS-Brazilian c.bank  \highlight22\uc2 intervenes \highlight\uc2  as real slumps; Colombian peso drops on tax reform\b0\par\par\uc2 578 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 November 2019\par 21:01\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2019 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     * Real cuts losses after two \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par     * \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Colombia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  announces changes in tax reform\par     * Latin American stocks eye worst day in three months\par \par  (Updates prices; adds details, news items)\par     By Medha Singh and Uday Sampath Kumar\par     Nov 26 (Reuters) - Brazil's real slipped in volatile trading\par on Tuesday to a fresh record low, with the government\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  twice to stymie a slump in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , while a\par change in Colombia's tax reform raised worries of higher fiscal\par deficit.\par     The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Brazilian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  said it is selling dollars in the\par spot \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market for the second time, announcing an auction\par of another minimum lot $1 million after the real slumped as much\par as 1.2% to 4.2770 against the dollar.\par     While the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  hit a record low last week after foreign\par bidders effectively failed to show up at a "mega" oil auction\par this month, a fresh wave of selling on Tuesday followed comments\par from Economy Minister \f28 \uc2 \f2 Paulo Guedes\f28 \uc2 \f2  that he was not worried about\par the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's weakness.\par     "The markets understood that as a sign that further\par depreciation is not considered a threat by the government," said\par Wilson Ferrarezi, an economist at TS Lombard in Sao Paulo.\par     "The current level near 4.30 does not seem to be justified\par when we look at the fundamentals."    \par     In Colombia, the peso shed 1.1% as President Ivan\par Duque announced changes to his unpopular tax reform proposal\par which would cost the government some 3.2 trillion pesos ($931\par million).\par     Duque said the proposal will be modified to return value\par added tax (VAT) to the poorest 20% of Colombians and lower\par contributions to healthcare by minimum wage pensioners.\par \par     "The VAT rebate poses a risk to Colombia's fiscal stance,"\par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Christian Lawrence\f28 \uc2 \f2 , Senior Market Strategist, LatAm \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at\par Rabobank in New York said.\par     "Any sort of major announcement like this is going to spark\par some worries, particularly when it seemed to come out of the\par blue."\par     Still, Colombian unions and student groups planned a seventh\par day of anti-government protest on Wednesday.\par     Mexico's peso continued to weaken after revised data\par on Monday showed Latin America's second largest economy was in a\par technical recession in the first half of the year. \par     The government said on Tuesday it would spend 859 billion\par pesos ($44.3 billion) in the first phase of an ambitious\par infrastructure plan underwritten by the private sector, to prop\par up the stagnating economy.\par     The broader sentiment was also dented by caution around the\par ongoing U.S.-China trade talks. The region's stocks\par  lost about 2%, set for their steepest fall in\par three months.   \par  ($1 = 19.3710 Mexican pesos)\par  ($1 = 3,433.94 Colombian pesos)\par \par     Key Latin American stock indexes and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at 1933 GMT:\par     \par  Stock indexes 