\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1- \highlight22\uc2 Colombia \highlight\uc2  cenbank head rules out  \highlight22\uc2 intervening \highlight\uc2  if peso falls\b0\par\par\uc2 461 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 September 2018\par 15:35\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2018 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (adds more quotes, background)\par \par \uc2 By Karin Strohecker\par \par \uc2 LONDON, Sept 4 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Colombia\b0 \highlight\uc2  has no plans to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets even if the peso depreciates by 10-15 percent, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Juan Jose Echavarria told Reuters on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Like most emerging \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Colombia's peso has weakened this year as the dollar has strengthened amid steady rate rises by the \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve\uc2 . But the peso's falls have been far less severe than peers in Argentina, Turkey, South Africa or Mexico.\par \par \uc2 While there are fears Colombia's big current account deficit makes it vulnerable to contagion, Echavarria ruled out \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "Absolutely not, even if we have a devaluation of 10 or 15 percent," he said in an interview. "10 percent (devaluation) is no problem at all."\par \par \uc2 Echavarria acknowledged that Colombia ranked high up on the list of countries suffering from a large current account deficit as a share of gross domestic product, together with peers Argentina, Turkey and South Africa that have come under increasing pressure recently. However, Colombia's trend had been in the right direction, he said.\par \par \uc2 "If you had a huge shock like Colombia had two or three years ago, you need to look at both - the level and the adjustment - two years ago it was 6.5 percent of GDP, now it is 3.2 percent."\par \par \uc2 The governor said he did not at this stage envisage raising interest rates either, even next year, but added that "a lot depends on the exchange rate".\par \par \uc2 A Reuters poll last week showed analysts expected the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep its benchmark interest rate unchanged at 4.25 percent at least until the first quarter of next year and predicted a rise to 4.75 percent by the end of 2019.\par \par \uc2 Asked if he shared the view of Finance Minister Alberto Carrasquilla - who took office at the beginning of August - that the fiscal outlook for Latin America's fourth-largest economy was worrying, Echavarria said the fiscal situation was "very manageable".\par \par \uc2 "It is a new government, and new governments always look carefully at the numbers," he said.\par \par \uc2 Echavarria also saw no reason for the U.S. Fed to be swayed in its policy decisions by the woes of emerging markets.\par \par \uc2 "They have to do their job, every country has to do their job. I don't think (Fed Chair Jerome) Powell will increase or decrease interest rates because of emerging markets. The best thing for emerging markets is that the Fed does what they need to do for inflation and growth." (Reporting by Karin Strohecker; Writing by Sujata Rao and Karin Strohecker Editing by Andrew Heavens and Peter Graff)\par \par \uc2 Released: 2018-9-7T13:35:54.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020180904ee9402509\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
