\par\par \b \uc2 EMERGING MARKETS-Brazil stocks rise after Kroton says considering Est\u225\'00\'E1cio deal\b0\par\par\uc2 506 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Juni 2016\par 22:38\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (Updates prices, adds Mexico and Colombia)\par     By Bruno Federowski and Paula Arend Laier\par     SAO PAULO, June 2 (Reuters) - Brazilian equities rose on\par Thursday after \f28 \uc2 \f2 Kroton Educacional SA\f28 \uc2 \f2  said it was considering a\par purchase of rival \f28 \uc2 \f2 Est\u225\'00\'E1cio Participa\u231\'00\'E7\u245\'00\'F5es SA\f28 \uc2 \f2 , boosting shares of\par both education companies.\par     Kroton, the world's biggest private education\par company, jumped by as much as 14 percent. Est\u225\'00\'E1cio \par shot up more than 20 percent, its biggest daily gain since\par October 2008.\par     Brazil's benchmark Bovespa stock index outperformed\par markets in the region, rising 1.8 percent. Mexico's peso fell to\par another three-month low while Colombia's peso posted its biggest\par rise in nearly a month.\par     Hopes that Brazil's interim President \f28 \uc2 \f2 Michel Temer\f28 \uc2 \f2  could\par have an easier time gathering lawmaker support than his\par predecessor, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Dilma Rousseff\f28 \uc2 \f2 , also fostered appetite for\par Brazilian assets. \par     The lower house of Congress, in a first round vote on\par Thursday, approved a constitutional amendment that would free up\par about 120 billion reais in funds currently earmarked for special\par purposes. \par     Backed by Temer's government, the lower house also\par sanctioned pay raises for military and civil servants that will\par cost about 4 billion reais ($1.1 billion) this year. \par     "This points in the opposite direction of fiscal austerity\par but could also increase governability. In net terms, it might\par not be so negative," said SLW brokerage trader Jo\u227\'00\'E3o Paulo de\par Gracia Corr\u234\'00\'EAa.\par     In Mexico, the peso tumbled for a second day after\par labor market data from the United States, its biggest trading\par partner, suggested the U.S. economy is regaining momentum and\par boosted chances for another hike in U.S. interest rates this\par summer. \par     The peso fell to as low as 18.674 per dollar, its lowest\par since mid-February, when the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to support\par the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Colombia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's peso, on the other hand, jumped 0.9\par percent, its biggest increase since mid-May, lifted by higher\par oil prices. \par     \par     Key Latin American stock indexes and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at 1957 GMT:\par  Stock indexes                             daily %     YTD %\par                                Latest       change    change\par  \f28 \uc2 \f2 MSCI\f28 \uc2 \f2  Emerging Markets            809.47      0.28      1.65\par                                                     \par  MSCI LatAm                      2062.54      0.87     11.74\par                                                     \par  Brazil Bovespa                 49884.45      1.78     15.07\par                                                     \par  Mexico IPC                     45515.13     -0.09      5.90\par                                                     \par  Chile IPSA                      3921.90      -0.2      6.57\par                                                     \par  Chile IGPA                     19337.25     -0.14      6.53\par                                                     \par  Argentina MerVal               12709.40      0.66      8.86\par                                                     \par  Colombia IGBC                   9695.06      0.29  