\par\par \b \uc2 INTERVIEW-Colombia  \highlight22\uc2 forex \highlight\uc2  measures not needed as problems short-term -Meisel\b0\par\par\uc2 447 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 August 2015\par 21:51\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 (Adds quotes, background)\par \par \uc2 By Nelson Bocanegra\par \par \uc2 BOGOTA, Aug 26 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem \b \highlight22\uc2 Colombia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rising inflation and the precipitous fall in the peso \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is unnecessary, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  board member Adolfo Meisel said on Wednesday, because both reflect transitory pressures.\par \par \uc2 Meisel, one of seven members of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's policy-setting board, said that although inflation will be above the bank's 2 to 4 percent target range this year, he expects it will close 2016 at between 3 and 4 percent.\par \par \uc2 The peso has depreciated 65.3 percent in the last 12 months.\par \par \uc2 "For there to be \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the exchange rate there would have to be liquidity problems in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market or speculation, which we aren't seeing at this moment," Meisel told Reuters in an interview at his office in Bogota.\par \par \uc2 "So long as much of this is temporary, prices correct themselves, the devaluation is just this once, we will not have to take measures," Meisel added.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  continues to reach record lows; it closed at 3,261.60 pesos to the dollar on Wednesday. Analysts say the effect of continued depreciation on inflation may hasten hikes in the benchmark interest rate, which policymakers have held steady for 12 straight months.\par \par \uc2 A weak \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  compared with the dollar is key to controlling Colombia's widening current account deficit, Meisel said.\par \par \uc2 Growth outlooks for Latin America's fourth-largest economy have been curbed by falling oil revenue and accelerating inflation, which reached 4.46 percent in the 12 months to July.\par \par \uc2 The bank board's vote to keep the 4.5 percent rate unchanged at its meeting last week was divided - with some members in favor of a raise.\par \par \uc2 Board member Carlos Cano said this week that the rate should have been hiked to keep inflation within the target.\par \par \uc2 Low prices for crude, Colombia's biggest export and source of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 , explain the sharp drop in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the bank board said in a statement after the vote.\par \par \uc2 Meisel said there is increased risk to economic growth and for higher inflation.\par \par \uc2 "These risks have gone up, but there is an uncertainty because if there is economic deceleration that could contribute to a lower inflation," he said, adding that he sees the economy growing close to 3 percent this year and next.\par \par \uc2 "I'm still optimistic," he said. "I think the industrial sector will help in the second half of the year, I think non-energy exports will too." (Reporting by Nelson Bocanegra; Writing by Julia Symmes Cobb; Editing by Leslie Adler)\par \par \uc2 Released: 2015-8-29T19:51:06.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020150826eb8q00pb5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
