\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Colombia \highlight\uc2 's  \highlight22\uc2 Forex Intervention \highlight\uc2  Not Weakening Peso -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,468 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Juli 2014\par 17:34\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 11:34 EDT - On Wall Street, an oft-repeated line is "don't fight the Fed," as the US \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  pumps money into the economy. \uc2 Colombia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , though, apparently doesn't have as much pull in its markets. In late June the bank said it was doubling its official dollar purchases to $2B between July and September to force the peso weaker to help exporters. Board member Mauricio Cardenas declared: "The peso's current exchange rate is not good for the country." Three weeks later, COP not only isn't weaker as \uc2 Colombia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was hoping, it's 1.5% stronger at 1,857/USD as investors bet on continued heavy dollar inflows. (dan.molinski@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 11:32 EDT - Last week's Canada jobs report marked the end of a 6-year run in which the comparable Canadian unemployment rate had been below that of the US. \uc2 Canadian government\uc2  officials have boasted about the country's superior job-creation record after the recession. That message may no longer apply when compared to the US, says TD Bank after looking at metrics such as the participation rate among people 25-54. Labor-market slack had been more significant in the US, but that's now converging. TD adds recent strength in the Canadian dollar versus the greenback "belies the convergence" in the two countries' labor markets. (paul.vieira@wsj.com; @paulvieira)\par \par \uc2 11:29 EDT - United's (UAL) continued struggles to complete its merger integration with Continental and its lagging margin performance could spark interest from activist investors, says \uc2 Stifel Nicolaus\uc2 . It thinks activists could try to force management to adopt a more-aggressive strategy to restructure hubs and UAL's domestic network, a turnaround effort that would go beyond the company's current 4-year plan to boost revenue and cut costs. While Stifel has confidence in management, it does admit UAL "appears to fit the criteria of what certain activists have been looking for recently to identify opportunities" in other industries. UAL CEO Jeff Smisek said in a recent interview his board is "supportive" of management's turnaround plan. As other airplane stocks rise, UAL is down 1% after last week's 15% rebound. (susan.carey@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 11:28 EDT - Venezuelan officials have been promising measures including an overhaul to their complex \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  system to turn around a moribund economy, but delays are keeping investors guessing over what kind of political clout President Maduro has to pull off painful adjustments. Jefferies says it's looking for clues in the ruling Socialist party's convention later this month, "with pragmatic measures dependent upon a consolidation of support for President Maduro." Bond investors looking for high yields have helped support Venezuelan debt prices, but Jefferies warns further delays in implementing economic measures may force market participants to become skeptical. (kejal.vyas@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 11:28 EDT - For central bankers, what's the difference between a risk and a vulnerability? Bank of Canada Governor Stephen Poloz, known for his use of metaphor to explain monetary policy, explained in an interview with WSJ how he and his team brainstormed different metaphors to explain the difference ahead of the bank's June financial stability report. They settled on a tree in a backyard with a crack, or a vulnerability, facing the potential of a storm strong enough to knock it down -- a risk. When BoC makes its regular policy announcement and issues its monetary policy report Wednesday, there's little doubt Poloz will hold the overnight rate at 1%. The greater suspense lies in his choice of metaphor. (elena.cherney@WSJ.com; @ElenaCherney)\par \par \uc2 11:23 EDT - \uc2 Barclays\uc2  expects French drug maker \uc2 Sanofi\uc2  (SNY) to raise EPS growth guidance when it reports 2Q earnings July 31. SNY management has been too "conservative" with its current 4%-7% EPS growth guidance at constant exchange rates, a position that's hurt SNY's share price, \uc2 Barclays\uc2  says. "The stock trades on a heavy discount to the sector due to growth and margin concerns that we believe are excessive. Given a sector like growth profile we believe \uc2 Sanofi\uc2  should be trading in line with the sector and expect quarterly earnings to drive a closing of the multiple gap." \uc2 Barclays\uc2  expects one key sales driver -- insulin brand Lantus -- to return to normal sales growth of about 15% after a tough 1Q. SNY up 0.9% at $52.10. (jeanne.whalen@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 11:20 EDT - Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy has become increasingly controversial, given its length and intensity, Goldman Sach's economist Alberto Ramos says. Heavy \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  started in May 2013 and still taking place despite the now-benign global emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  environment, helping to keep the Brazilian real increasingly overvalued. That, in turn, is "keeping the economy weak and unbalanced," Ramos says. Some economists already say Brazil's GDP may grow less than 1% in 2014. (Luciana.Magalhaes@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 11:19 EDT - The US machine tool business remains soft. Orders for the metal-cutting equipment -- used in a wide range of manufacturing -- were down 17.5% to $351.9M in May, compared to a year-ago, according to the Association for Manufacturing Technology. So far this year, orders are down 1.4% compared to 2013. The soft order books in the early part of this year are expected to be offset by a stronger second half of the year. (tim.aeppel@wsj.com; @timaeppel)\par \par \uc2 11:18 EDT - Whiting's (WLL) $3.8B deal for fellow Williston Basin oil producer Kodiak (KOG), which valued the latter's stock at slightly below Friday's closing price, shows "there is sometimes more to M&A than just numbers," says \uc2 Wells Fargo\uc2 . It notes CEO Lynn Peterson has much of his net worth in KOG, and that management had tried unsuccessfully for years to sell the company. "Putting ourselves in those shoes, we would be willing to take a little less to lock in the wealth and take the risk off the table," says Wells. WLL climbs 6.2% while KOG gains 4.4% at $14.86 as both hit all-time highs. (ryan.dezember@wsj.com; @RyanDezember)\par \par \uc2 11:15 EDT - Capital Economics says the deceleration in home sales in Canada -- yearly gain of 4.4% in June vs 4.6% in May -- will likely continue, with the past decline in the existing home sales-to-listings ratio pointing to a further slowdown during 2H. The recent drop in mortgage costs might spark a pick-up in home sales during the next few months, and that could delay the onset of price weakness until next year, firm says. But it's been calling for a 25% correction in the market for more than three years, and is sticking to that. "[We] still believe that the housing market is headed for a major correction over the longer term," CapEcon says. (don.curren@wsj.com @dbcurren)\par \par \uc2 11:13 EDT - The import tariffs leveled Friday by the US on South Korean steel for the oil-and-gas industry will firm up US prices--and the bottom lines of domestic producers--but won't fundamentally alter the market, say analysts. The duties on South Korean steel pipe and tube of an average 13%, or roughly $100/ton, "can probably be absorbed somewhat by the Koreans' cost structure," says John Packard, publisher of Steel Market Update. But expect South Korea, which has a 15-20% market share, to keep exporting steel pipe and tube to the US, predicts KeyBanc. It thinks the decision should help prices be as much as $110/ton higher than early-2014 levels. (john.miller@wsj.com; @wsjmiller)\par \par \uc2 11:11 EDT - \uc2 Citigroup\uc2 's (C) 2Q earnings were boosted $610M by the bank's release of some of its bad-loan reserves, an amount slightly less than the $646M it released in 1Q. Citi and other big national banks have been releasing reserves every quarter for more than four years because of improvements in credit quality, and those amounts go directly into the bank's operating income. Observers have been expecting reserve releases to dwindle, since credit quality isn't improving as quickly now, but thus far the big national banks' 2Q releases are holding fairly steady compared with 1Q. (michael.rapoport@wsj.com; @rapoportwsj) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par To contact the Market Talk Editors, \par Kevin Kingsbury, 212 416-2354, kevin.kingsbury@wsj.com \par Cynthia Lin, 212 416-4403, Cynthia.lin@wsj.com \par John Shipman, 212 416-2181, john.shipman@wsj.com \par Patrick Sullivan, 212 416-2326, Patrick.sullivan@wsj.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 July 14, 2014 11:34 ET (15:34 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140714ea7e0028a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
