\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Colombia \highlight\uc2 's  \highlight22\uc2 Forex Intervention \highlight\uc2  Not Weakening Peso -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 145 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Juli 2014\par 17:34\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 11:34 EDT - On Wall Street, an oft-repeated line is "don't fight the Fed," as the US \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  pumps money into the economy. \uc2 Colombia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , though, apparently doesn't have as much pull in its markets. In late June the bank said it was doubling its official dollar purchases to $2B between July and September to force the peso weaker to help exporters. Board member Mauricio Cardenas declared: "The peso's current exchange rate is not good for the country." Three weeks later, COP not only isn't weaker as \uc2 Colombia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was hoping, it's 1.5% stronger at 1,857/USD as investors bet on continued heavy dollar inflows. (dan.molinski@wsj.com) \par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 July 14, 2014 11:34 ET (15:34 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140714ea7e002a7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
