\par\par \b \uc2 Zinc Prices Up on Higher Demand, Falling Supply -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,362 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Juli 2014\par 20:45\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 14:45 EDT - Not all mining and metals commodities are treading water. Zinc prices for example are up 22% in the past year, and shares prices in pure-play zinc companies are up 30% thanks to "continued global demand growth, higher mine production costs, and declining inventory levels," says Ignace Proot, an analyst for Bernstein. Proot says Bernstein remains "bullish on zinc", because it's used in so many consumer goods and because of a "structural shortage" due to mine depletion and closures. (john.miller@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 14:42 EDT - More coal miners are likely on the verge of cutbacks, Sterne Agee says. Prices are so bad for metallurgic coal that likely 40% to 50% of the global export production gets produced at a loss, analyst Ray Young estimates. Recent deals have set a 3Q benchmark price of $120 a ton, which isn't enough for most US producers. The industry has recently announced production cutbacks between 15M and 20M tons and more announcements are likely to come in the weeks and months ahead, Sterne Agee says. (tim.puko@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 14:39 EDT - Private equity firm TPG is quietly incubating an internal hedge fund, its second-ever, to run a macro strategy, WSJ reported today. TPG and its partners will put as much as $100M into the fund, with hopes to raise more next year. That's a small fraction of the commitment from Blackstone (BX), and its clients, to its multi-strategy Blackstone hedge fund that will start as soon as the fall, as WSJ reported earlier this week. Each Blackstone team, of which there could eventually be several dozen, will have about $150M to trade, sources said. If prior experience is any guide however, it'll be the investment performance that proves whether or not the efforts are successful, rather than day-one cash. (rob.copeland@wsj.com; @realrobcopeland)\par \par \uc2 14:38 EDT - Fiat Chrysler may soon be listed in New York, but its post-merger financial statements will adopt accounting standards mostly used by companies outside the U.S., according to a new regulatory filing. It's a small change but one that's likely to clear up some earlier confusion. Right now, Chrysler reports using U.S. GAAP standards, while Fiat uses IFRS, which is common for European companies. After the merger, they'll just use one method -- IFRS. (christina.rogers@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 14:33 EDT - In a move that demonstrates the futility of official \b \highlight22\uc2 forex intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  during a driven market, \b \highlight22\uc2 Colombia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's peso ended today at COP1,843/USD, its strongest since May 2013 as the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  goes up right when \uc2 Colombia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it would go down. Late last month the bank announced a doubling of dollar purchases aimed at weakening COP, to $2B in 3Q versus $1B in 2Q. But the dollar inflows were just too strong after today's positive US jobs report, which is boosting interest in emerging markets, combined with a new Colombian GDP report that showed 1Q growth was a better-than-forecast 6.4% on-year. (dan.molinski@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 14:31 EDT - \uc2 Bank of Mexico\uc2 's June survey of economists finds a downgrade in their median estimate for GDP this year to 2.7% from 2.8%, which puts the number in line with the Finance Ministry. But brokerage Invex says there's a silver lining: the worst of the slowdown is likely over. "The principle risks to growth continue to be public insecurity, weakness in the internal market and fragility in the external market. The majority of those surveyed expect a better business climate in the next six months and think it's a good moment to invest," the brokerage says, adding that the economic situation is also looking up for 2015. (laurence.iliff@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 14:19 EDT - \uc2 Alcoa\uc2  (AA) shares end higher despite disclosing that new regulations on warehousing could put more aluminum on the market and deflate prices for raw aluminum, still its biggest business. Analysts say that's an indication that its strategy of positioning itself as a producer of downstream aluminum products for aerospace and automotive industries could be working. Shares are near a three-year high. \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2  recently raised target price to $15 from $10 per share. AA's 2Q earnings Tuesday will be closely watched. It's expected to post EPS of 12 cents, after a loss of 11 cents per share a year ago. AA ends up 0.9% to $14.98. (john.miller@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 14:17 EDT - An Illinois Supreme Court ruling Friday casts a pall over last year's overhaul of the state pension system. Court rules state contributions to retiree medical insurance are a pension benefit and can't be cut. Legal fight over larger pension cuts remains, but Senate President John Cullerton says: "If the court's decision is predictive, the challenge of reforming our pension systems will remain." Others are more bluntly with Christopher Mooney, director of Institute of Government and Public Affairs at \uc2 University of Illinois\uc2 , saying: "We're basically back to square one." (mark.peters@wsj.com; @mrmmpeters)\par \par \uc2 14:05 EDT - SunTrust (STI) puts another mortgage investigation behind it, but it's not out of the woods. STI agreed to pay up to $320M to end a federal criminal probe of its participation in a mortgage-modification program. The settlement follows an agreement last month with the Justice Dept in which STI agreed to pay $968M in fines and consumer relief over allegations that it mishandled servicing of borrowers' loans and failed to adhere to the underwriting standards for a federal insurance program. STI still faces a DOJ investigation over loans it sold to \uc2 Fannie Mae\uc2  and \uc2 Freddie Mac\uc2 . STI closed up 1.4% at $40.76. (andrewr.johnson@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 13:58 EDT - After losing ground in morning trading in the wake of the unexpectedly robust US jobs data, the Canadian dollar has rebounded. USD/CAD zoomed higher after the payrolls report, but has since given up those gains, and then some. Market players credit a "Buy North America" sentiment as a big driver of the CAD's recovery, as investors interpret strength in the US economy as a driver of stronger Canadian growth. That's playing into heavy selling of the euro and other non-North American \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the Canadian dollar. USD/CAD is last down 0.3% at 1.0631 from a post-payroll high of 1.0681. Major support is seen around 1.0600. (don.curren@wsj.com; @dbcurren)\par \par \uc2 13:46 EDT - "Could your 4th of July burger be topped with mozzarella or provolone this year instead of a slice of American cheese?" asks the USDA in a dairy report. Good question. Availability of Italian cheeses such as mozzarella, parmesan and provolone has increased in the US to 14.9 pounds per person in 2012, up from 2.1 pounds in 1970. American cheese has been steady since 2005 at 13 pounds per person, USDA report says. Buon appetito! (leslie.josephs@wsj.com; @lesliejosephs)\par \par \uc2 13:33 EDT - Another central banker weighs into the debate over bitcoin, and it's probably not what the industry wants to hear. "Should anyone else be allowed to create money other than a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ? There is an issue of sovereignty at stake," he told attendees at a Dublin bitcoin gathering Thursday. In an interview after the speech, Gareth Murphy, the \uc2 Bank of Ireland\uc2 's director of market supervision, said: "To the extent that the bitcoin industry was seeking to engage with the wider economy, they shouldn't be surprised if Moses came down from the mountain with the law." (amir.mizroch@wsj.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par To contact the Market Talk Editors, \par Kevin Kingsbury, 212 416-2354, kevin.kingsbury@wsj.com \par Cynthia Lin, 212 416-4403, Cynthia.lin@wsj.com \par John Shipman, 212 416-2181, john.shipman@wsj.com \par Patrick Sullivan, 212 416-2326, Patrick.sullivan@wsj.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 July 03, 2014 14:45 ET (18:45 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140703ea730031u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
