\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Colombia \highlight\uc2  Peso Hits 13-Month High Despite  \highlight22\uc2 Interventions \highlight\uc2  -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,382 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Juli 2014\par 20:33\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 14:33 EDT - In a move that demonstrates the futility of official \b \highlight22\uc2 forex intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  during a driven market, \b \highlight22\uc2 Colombia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's peso ended today at COP1,843/USD, its strongest since May 2013 as the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  goes up right when \uc2 Colombia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it would go down. Late last month the bank announced a doubling of dollar purchases aimed at weakening COP, to $2B in 3Q versus $1B in 2Q. But the dollar inflows were just too strong after today's positive US jobs report, which is boosting interest in emerging markets, combined with a new Colombian GDP report that showed 1Q growth was a better-than-forecast 6.4% on-year. (dan.molinski@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 14:17 EDT - An Illinois Supreme Court ruling Friday casts a pall over last year's overhaul of the state pension system. Court rules state contributions to retiree medical insurance are a pension benefit and can't be cut. Legal fight over larger pension cuts remains, but Senate President John Cullerton says: "If the court's decision is predictive, the challenge of reforming our pension systems will remain." Others are more bluntly with Christopher Mooney, director of Institute of Government and Public Affairs at \uc2 University of Illinois\uc2 , saying: "We're basically back to square one." (mark.peters@wsj.com; @mrmmpeters)\par \par \uc2 13:58 EDT - After losing ground in morning trading in the wake of the unexpectedly robust US jobs data, the Canadian dollar has rebounded. USD/CAD zoomed higher after the payrolls report, but has since given up those gains, and then some. Market players credit a "Buy North America" sentiment as a big driver of the CAD's recovery, as investors interpret strength in the US economy as a driver of stronger Canadian growth. That's playing into heavy selling of the euro and other non-North American \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the Canadian dollar. USD/CAD is last down 0.3% at 1.0631 from a post-payroll high of 1.0681. Major support is seen around 1.0600. (don.curren@wsj.com; @dbcurren)\par \par \uc2 13:43 EDT - Chilean President Michelle Bachelet's approval rating rose two percentage points to 58% in June from the month before, pollster Adimark says, as those polled said the president seemed more credible and has the abilities to find solutions to problems facing the nation. Bachelet took office in March for a second term as president. In the same timeframe during her first term, she had only a 44% approval rating, the pollster notes. (robert.kozak@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 12:59 EDT - \uc2 Bank of America Merrill Lynch\uc2 , which has long predicted the Bank of Canada would wait until 1Q 2016 to hike rates, acknowledges the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may act sooner if exports continue to power ahead. While Canada's auto sector continues to face competitiveness challenges, momentum in sectors tied to US growth seems to be picking up, so although BoAML is still looking for rate hikes to start in 1Q 2016 "there is a risk that the BOC may not need to wait so long" if surprisingly strong export momentum seen in the May report continues. (nirmala.menon@wsj.com; @NirmalaMenon)\par \par \uc2 12:47 EDT - Though a debt restructuring by the \uc2 Puerto Rico Electric Power Authority\uc2  might buy the utility some breathing room, a bigger question looms: how will Prepa undertake deeper reforms, including updating inefficient power plants? Two recently enacted laws raise questions about who will oversee such changes. A May energy-reform law calls for the creation of an independent regulatory commission to supervise the utility. But a just-passed restructuring law allows for the appointment of an emergency administrator. "The relationship between both laws is not entirely clear," says Sergio Marxuach, policy director at the Center for a New Economy in San Juan. (arian.campo-flores@wsj.com; @acampoflores)\par \par \uc2 12:42 EDT - Among the many uncertainties surrounding the possible restructuring of the \uc2 Puerto Rico Electric Power Authority\uc2  is what an overhaul would mean for the island's electricity rates, which are more than double the US average and have been a drag on Puerto Rico's economy. If the utility succeeds in restructuring its debt and lowering its payments, it could pass on savings to residents and businesses. On the other hand, if the power authority is unable to renew lines of credit to buy imported oil, which accounts for more than 60% of its energy production, electricity rates might increase. (arian.campo-flores@wsj.com; @acampoflores)\par \par \uc2 12:39 EDT - \uc2 Bank of America Merrill Lynch\uc2  raises Canada's growth forecasts for 2Q and 2014 in the wake of a strong May trade report. BoAML now expects 2Q GDP growth of 2.1% annualized, from its prior forecast of 1.9%. As a result, its forecast for all of 2014 was raised a tick to 2.1%. (nirmala.menon@wsj.com; @NirmalaMenon)\par \par \uc2 1636 GMT [Dow Jones]--How big is a bazooka? Think at least a trillion. It seems to be the policy maker's number of choice. European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Mario Draghi said Thursday that the take-up for the ECB's new targeted lending \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  could reach EUR1 trillion. But it's not the first package to seek to impress markets with that eye-catching trillion tag. In April 2009, when the global economy was on the precipice, the G-20 cobbled together a package "to restore credit, growth and jobs" that came in at $1.1 trillion. In May 2010, when the Greek crisis erupted, Europe put together a deal that weighed in at EUR750 billion -- then around $1 trillion. Then there were the ultimately fruitless efforts to leverage the funds available to the \uc2 European Financial Stability Facility\uc2  in 2011. How much firepower might have been generated? Oh, about EUR1 trillion. And the last time Mr. Draghi offered cheap long-term funding to banks, in late 2012, the \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  provided--you guessed it--just over EUR1 trillion. If you're going for shock and awe, billions just don't cut it any more. (richard.barley@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 12:33 EDT - \uc2 Moody's\uc2  downgrades $290M in revenue bonds from \uc2 Howard University\uc2 , cutting the debt by two notches to Baa3 with a negative outlook. Ratings firm cites "precipitous deterioration" of \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  at the historically black college's hospital, with a $37M operating loss in fiscal 2014 and long-lasting upheaval in its leadership ranks. Though the hospital's administration is trying to right the ship, \uc2 Moody's\uc2  says, it's not moving fast enough. The fact that the university still needs to figure out financing for new student housing could pressure the bottom line further. (melissa.korn@wsj.com; @melissakorn)\par \par \uc2 12:12 EDT - Near-record to record-setting rainfall last month largely eradicated lingering drought in the Midwest, USDA meteorologist Brad Rippey says. By July 1, drought covered just 5% of US area where soybeans are grown, and only 8% of the corn area, Rippey says. Corn and soybean futures have traded at multi-month lows this week, in part due to the favorable weather. During the four weeks through July 1, the portion of the US afflicted with drought fell 3.31 percentage points to 34.01%. "Nevertheless, drought still covers a substantial portion of the central and southern Plains and the western US," Rippey says, adding that 79% of California, the nation's biggest agricultural producer by revenue, is suffering from extreme to exceptional drought. (david.kesmodel@wsj.com; @davidkesmodel)\par \par \uc2 1554 GMT [Dow Jones]--The \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's plan to offer banks cheap loans in a bid to boost lending to small businesses is likely to reduce high-yield bond issuance, say credit strategists at \uc2 Bank of America Merrill Lynch\uc2 . The bank notes that previous ECB policy measures have indirectly resulted in a scarcity of certain credit assets. For example, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  doled out more than EUR1 trillion ($1.4 trillion) of cheap loans to euro-zone banks at the peak of the region's debt crisis, causing a sharp drop in senior bank-bond issuance. (ben.edwards@wsj.com @debtnerd)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 July 03, 2014 14:33 ET (18:33 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140703ea7300317\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
