\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Colombia \highlight\uc2  Peso Hits 13-Month High Despite  \highlight22\uc2 Interventions \highlight\uc2  -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,426 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Juli 2014\par 20:33\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 14:33 EDT - In a move that demonstrates the futility of official \b \highlight22\uc2 forex intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  during a driven market, \b \highlight22\uc2 Colombia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's peso ended today at COP1,843/USD, its strongest since May 2013 as the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  goes up right when \uc2 Colombia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it would go down. Late last month the bank announced a doubling of dollar purchases aimed at weakening COP, to $2B in 3Q versus $1B in 2Q. But the dollar inflows were just too strong after today's positive US jobs report, which is boosting interest in emerging markets, combined with a new Colombian GDP report that showed 1Q growth was a better-than-forecast 6.4% on-year. (dan.molinski@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 14:31 EDT - \uc2 Bank of Mexico\uc2 's June survey of economists finds a downgrade in their median estimate for GDP this year to 2.7% from 2.8%, which puts the number in line with the Finance Ministry. But brokerage Invex says there's a silver lining: the worst of the slowdown is likely over. "The principle risks to growth continue to be public insecurity, weakness in the internal market and fragility in the external market. The majority of those surveyed expect a better business climate in the next six months and think it's a good moment to invest," the brokerage says, adding that the economic situation is also looking up for 2015. (laurence.iliff@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 14:19 EDT - \uc2 Alcoa\uc2  (AA) shares end higher despite disclosing that new regulations on warehousing could put more aluminum on the market and deflate prices for raw aluminum, still its biggest business. Analysts say that's an indication that its strategy of positioning itself as a producer of downstream aluminum products for aerospace and automotive industries could be working. Shares are near a three-year high. \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2  recently raised target price to $15 from $10 per share. AA's 2Q earnings Tuesday will be closely watched. It's expected to post EPS of 12 cents, after a loss of 11 cents per share a year ago. AA ends up 0.9% to $14.98. (john.miller@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 14:17 EDT - An Illinois Supreme Court ruling Friday casts a pall over last year's overhaul of the state pension system. Court rules state contributions to retiree medical insurance are a pension benefit and can't be cut. Legal fight over larger pension cuts remains, but Senate President John Cullerton says: "If the court's decision is predictive, the challenge of reforming our pension systems will remain." Others are more bluntly with Christopher Mooney, director of Institute of Government and Public Affairs at \uc2 University of Illinois\uc2 , saying: "We're basically back to square one." (mark.peters@wsj.com; @mrmmpeters)\par \par \uc2 14:05 EDT - SunTrust (STI) puts another mortgage investigation behind it, but it's not out of the woods. STI agreed to pay up to $320M to end a federal criminal probe of its participation in a mortgage-modification program. The settlement follows an agreement last month with the Justice Dept in which STI agreed to pay $968M in fines and consumer relief over allegations that it mishandled servicing of borrowers' loans and failed to adhere to the underwriting standards for a federal insurance program. STI still faces a DOJ investigation over loans it sold to \uc2 Fannie Mae\uc2  and \uc2 Freddie Mac\uc2 . STI closed up 1.4% at $40.76. (andrewr.johnson@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 13:58 EDT - After losing ground in morning trading in the wake of the unexpectedly robust US jobs data, the Canadian dollar has rebounded. USD/CAD zoomed higher after the payrolls report, but has since given up those gains, and then some. Market players credit a "Buy North America" sentiment as a big driver of the CAD's recovery, as investors interpret strength in the US economy as a driver of stronger Canadian growth. That's playing into heavy selling of the euro and other non-North American \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the Canadian dollar. USD/CAD is last down 0.3% at 1.0631 from a post-payroll high of 1.0681. Major support is seen around 1.0600. (don.curren@wsj.com; @dbcurren)\par \par \uc2 13:46 EDT - "Could your 4th of July burger be topped with mozzarella or provolone this year instead of a slice of American cheese?" asks the USDA in a dairy report. Good question. Availability of Italian cheeses such as mozzarella, parmesan and provolone has increased in the US to 14.9 pounds per person in 2012, up from 2.1 pounds in 1970. American cheese has been steady since 2005 at 13 pounds per person, USDA report says. Buon appetito! (leslie.josephs@wsj.com; @lesliejosephs)\par \par \uc2 13:33 EDT - Another central banker weighs into the debate over bitcoin, and it's probably not what the industry wants to hear. "Should anyone else be allowed to create money other than a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ? There is an issue of sovereignty at stake," he told attendees at a Dublin bitcoin gathering Thursday. In an interview after the speech, Gareth Murphy, the \uc2 Bank of Ireland\uc2 's director of market supervision, said: "To the extent that the bitcoin industry was seeking to engage with the wider economy, they shouldn't be surprised if Moses came down from the mountain with the law." (amir.mizroch@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 13:19 EDT - This year's 1H "was a microcosm of the post-Great Financial Crisis era; the mantra of 'I'm so bearish, I'm bullish' lived on as bearish economic news was overpowered by an aggressively bullish policy response; growth again disappointed...and assets prices rose," writes BofA/Merrill strategist Michael Hartnett. Stock market "melt-up" he's been predicting has more room to go. "Bottom line: melt-up is not over; probably in the latter stages of the move; watch for weaker credit, weaker bank stocks and a stronger dollar for signs that it is closer to an end." (john.shipman@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 13:16 EDT - Some confusion today in the press over Uber's fate in London, where the taxi-hailing app faces a fight with the black cab industry. The body in charge of the city's transportation has long said Uber app is compliant with the law and referred the matter to UK's High Court. Today, the agency reiterated its stance -- the app can operate in the city -- but it must wait for criminal complaints filed by the London Taxi Drivers' Association against Uber drivers to settle first before High Court will rule on the matter. So Uber's ability to operate in London remains in limbo. At issue: whether the Uber app is the same as taxi meters, which calculate fares and are only used by licensed black cabs. (scott.austin@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 13:14 EDT - \uc2 Standard & Poor's\uc2  maintains its A+ rating for \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2  (BNP.FR) after the French bank pleads guilty to breaching US economic sanctions and is fined $8.97B. "We believe that the sanctions will not have a major detrimental impact on \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2 ' risks and business profile," it says. S&P, however, revises its assessment of the bank's capital to "moderate" from "adequate." The massive fine will have an impact on the bank's capital buffers -- BNP has said its core tier-one ratio would decrease to about 10% as of June-end. (noemie.bisserbe@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 13:11 EDT - Given the limited participation, typically doesn't take much to get US stock markets moving up in a pre-holiday, shortened session. Better-than-expected headline gain in June jobs report, along with generally positive trends within much of the unemployment gauges, proves to be more than enough to fuel a rally. Five stocks -- \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2 , Visa, CAT, Exxon and \uc2 Chevron\uc2  -- produce more than half the Dow's gain on its way to another record high and first close above 17000. DJIA up 92.02 to 17068.26, and Nasdaq Comp adds 28.19 to 4485.93. S&P 500 ends 10.82 higher at 1985.44, another record. (john.shipman@wsj.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par To contact the Market Talk Editors, \par Kevin Kingsbury, 212 416-2354, kevin.kingsbury@wsj.com \par Cynthia Lin, 212 416-4403, Cynthia.lin@wsj.com \par John Shipman, 212 416-2181, john.shipman@wsj.com \par Patrick Sullivan, 212 416-2326, Patrick.sullivan@wsj.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 July 03, 2014 14:33 ET (18:33 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140703ea730033n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
