\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Colombia \highlight\uc2  Gets Serious On  \highlight22\uc2 Forex Intervention \highlight\uc2  -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,489 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Juni 2014\par 15:41\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 9:41 EDT - \b \highlight22\uc2 Colombia\b0 \highlight\uc2  financial markets are back from a three-day weekend with the peso in focus after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said late Friday it will double its \b \highlight22\uc2 forex intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , whose strength vs USD keeps hurting exporters of coffee, bananas and products from other labor-intensive sectors. The bank said it will buy up to $2B in 3Q vs $1B in 1Q. COP, 3% higher this year, is slightly weaker today at 1885/USD vs 1883 Friday, but Bancolombia says it believes overall "the effect of these purchases on the direction and level of the nominal exchange rate is limited." (dan.molinski@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1341 GMT [Dow Jones]--The $886 billion Norwegian Government Pension Fund Global is set for a property buying spree, planning to spend 1% of its value annually through 2016 on real estate. The GPFG, the world's largest sovereign wealth fund, often referred to as the oil fund, said Tuesday its main targets were global cities like New York, London and Paris, but that it may also include big cities outside of Europe and the U.S. The aim is to build a global, but concentrated portfolio, with an ultimate target of 5% of the fund's value in real estate, from 1% at the end of 2013. So far, the fund has invested mainly in joint ventures with companies including French insurer AXA, asset manager \uc2 TIAA-CREF\uc2  and \uc2 Boston Properties\uc2 , but the fund said it would "prepare the organization for management of fully owned properties and a more active role in the development of our properties." The Oslo-based fund also considered larger ownership stakes in listed real estate companies and public-to-private transactions, and expected to expand its real estate organization to 200 people by 2016 and its total staff to 600. (kjetilmalkenes.hovland@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1337 GMT [Dow Jones] Central banks are placing new emphasis on expectations, conveying not only the outlook path of rates but also how bumpy the ride will be, says Steven Englander at \uc2 Citigroup\uc2 . BoE Governor Carney reversed part of the hawkishness at a recent speech and made reference to the MPC's surprise at how little risk was priced in by investors. "This was echoed by Fed Chair Yellen in her repeated references to two-way risk. Now the message is 'we are so committed to hitting the economic targets that we will let rates go where they must in order to get there'," says Mr. Englander. (chiara.albanese@wsj.com @chiaraalbanese)\par \par \uc2 9:36 EDT - Home price growth slowed more than expected in April, giving copper prices a bit of a knock. Prices have rallied in the last few weeks, as investors focus on improving Chinese data and shrug off a probe into illegal commodity-fueled financing practices at a major Chinese port. But strong housing data are needed from the U.S. to give the rally some legs, traders say. Stronger real estate data would indicate that increased demand for copper is just around the corner. But while the U.S. has shown improvements in jobs numbers and car sales, real estate data continues to lag. Copper is down 0.1% at $3.1405 on Comex. (ira.iosebashvili@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1333 GMT [Dow Jones]--Thanks to ceasefire in Ukraine and Russian President Vladimir Putin's request to rescind his earlier authorization to use military forces there, the ruble firmed to its strongest since mid-January of 33.78 versus the dollar Tuesday. Russia's local month-end tax payment along with strong oil prices both play into the ruble's hands. But can this strengthening be prolonged? ING says that even in case of further ruble firming the Russian unit is unlikely to be able to anchor within a range of 33.0-33.5 per dollar in the current fundamental environment. Russia is still on track to post its direst economic growth in a decade of around 0.5%, excluding the year of 2009 when it contracted under the global crisis burden. Apart from poor fundamentals, the ruble is likely to come under seasonal pressure. Russian companies are set to pay dividends within a month, which means that part of that many foreign and even local investors will prefer to convert into dollars or euros, bringing the ruble under pressure again. "So, follow the trend, but don't lose a bigger picture," ING says. (andrey.ostroukh@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 "Medical Costs for U.S. Employer-Based Coverage to Rise 6.8% Next Year- PwC Arm -- Market Talk," published at 0435 GMT incorrectly referred to the company giving the projection as PVC's Health Research Institute. It should read PwC's Health Research Institute.\par \par \uc2 1321 GMT [Dow Jones]--\uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2  reenters a long PLN/HUF position around 73.50, to target 76.50 with a stop down at 73.00. Last week the French bank exited its long PLN/HUF trade for a 1.3% profit as the leaked tape scandal hit the zloty hard, suggesting a wave of stop-losses were triggered and positioning reduced. With the political fallout in Poland now looking to have reached a climax with no real market implications, the bank climbs back in again. PLN/HUF now trades at 73.60 from 74.40 a week ago, and an 11-week highs of 74.75 on June 12. (gary.stride@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 9:04 EDT - Home prices in 20 U.S. cities rose 10.8% in the year ended in April, says S&P/Case-Shiller. That's down from 12.4% in March and below the 11.4% expected by forecasters. The report says the deceleration reflects a slowdown in Sunbelt cities. Where prices were once surging close to 30%, growth is now below 20%. Price appreciation around 10% is still a good return, and a pickup in home demand--evidenced by the 4.9% gain in May existing home sales--suggests home prices won't unravel. (kathleen.madigan@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1229 GMT [Dow Jones]--\uc2 Turkey \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Tuesday cut the one-week repo rate to 8.75% from 9.5%, but left its interest rate corridor intact and kept the overnight lending rate and overnight borrowing rate at 12% and 8%, respectively. "The rate cut was more dovish than we had expected, but by leaving the corridor unchanged, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  seems to have counterbalanced any potentially dismal impact on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," says Erste Securities. It notes that the decision is positive for short-term bond yields and further easing could be possible contingent on the inflation and global interest rate outlook. (yeliz.candemir@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1227 GMT [Dow Jones]--European Summary: A mixed session for the U.S. dollar, with sterling falling sharply after BoE Governor Mark Carney tempered the hawkish message of his recent Mansion House speech, while the euro side-stepped a soft German Ifo survey to end the session a touch firmer. Japan's PM Abe's unveiled the government's latest growth strategy which held no major surprises, and the JPY was largely unchanged. Turkey cut its benchmark rate by a larger-than-consensus 75 bps, the lira showed minimal reaction. Away from the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  the main European equity bourses lacked direction, trading just either side of flat, while core government bonds are a touch easier. (gary.stride@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1222 GMT [Dow Jones]--Treasurys are slightly higher, boosted by disappointing German business sentiment. Bank of England head Mark Carney said wage pressures remain tame, denting some concerns a rate hike could come sooner than many expect. New home sales and consumer confidence at 10 a.m. are main US data to watch. A $30bln sale of two-year Treasury notes is due at 1 p.m. The 10-year note is 3/32 higher, yielding 2.610%. Some traders expect the tight range of 2.55%-2.66% to hold in the short term. (min.zeng@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1207 GMT [Dow Jones]--\uc2 Turkey's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Tuesday lowered its benchmark one-week policy rate by 75 basis points to 8.75% from 9.50%, more than market consensus of 50bps cut. "Today's more aggressive rate cut is a further demonstration of the CBRT's instinctive pro-growth bias," says Nicholas Spiro, managing director at Spiro Sovereign Strategy. "The \uc2 Turkish \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's dovish policy belies its somewhat hawkish rhetoric," he adds. He notes that once again, the CBRT is using the cover of improved sentiment towards Turkish and EM assets to cut rates as sharply at it can despite Turkey's high inflation rate. "However the more the CBRT loosens monetary policy, the more its actions reinforce the perception that it's succumbing to political pressure," says Spiro. (yeliz.candemir@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 June 24, 2014 09:41 ET (13:41 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140624ea6o001z2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
