\par\par \b \uc2 *\uc2 Colombia  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  Raises Key Rate, As Expected, 25 Basis Points to 4.0%\b0\par\par\uc2 517 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Juni 2014\par 20:22\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 20 Jun 2014 14:30 ET *\uc2 Colombia \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Says Will Buy Up to $2 Billion in \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Market July-September\par \par \uc2 20 Jun 2014 15:19 ET  \b \highlight22\uc2 Colombia\b0 \highlight\uc2  Raises Rates, Doubles \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  Market\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  By Dan Molinski \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 BOGOTA--\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Colombia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  raised its key lending rate for a third straight month Friday to cool an expanding economy and said it will aggressively increase its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets to get its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to weaken against the dollar.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The bank's seven-member board said after its monthly meeting that it voted to increase its key interest rate 25 basis points to 4.0%. Economists widely expected the move, especially after the government released data Thursday that showed Colombia's economy expanded in the first quarter faster than analysts were forecasting.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In explaining its rate decision, the bank said that as the oil-driven economy picks up steam, consumer inflation is rising at a faster clip than was expected, and said that raising rates now, which can reduce consumer demand and temper inflationary pressures, will avoid the need for sharp adjustments in the future.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Taking that into account, as well as the lag-time that occurs before monetary policy actions affect inflation and growth, the board decided to increase the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rate," bank President Jose Dario Uribe told reporters.\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Colombia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's consumer inflation last year was 1.9%, one of its lowest rate in decades. But prices have climbed steadily and the annual rate through May is now 2.9% and is expected to go even higher before the end of the year. The bank's target inflation range is 2% to 4%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The bank also announced Friday that starting in July it will sharply increase its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets to force its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to weaken against the dollar, a move that could help Colombian exporters by making their products more competitive abroad. The bank said it will buy up to $2 billion from July through September, a doubling of the $1 billion limit it set for the April thru June period.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The peso's exchange rate, where it is right now, is not good for the country," Finance Minister Mauricio Cardenas, who is also one of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's seven board members, told reporters in explaining the bank's decision.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Colombia's peso ended Friday afternoon at 1,883 for a dollar, 2.6% stronger from where it began the year. Officials would like to see the peso weaken toward 2,000 for a dollar.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 President Juan Manuel Santos, who was elected June 15 to another four-year term, said last week Colombia's economy may grow 5.5% this year. During the first quarter, expansion reached 6.4% versus the same quarter of last year, with the growth being led by soaring investment in construction, mainly large infrastructure projects such as highways.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Write to Dan Molinski at dan.molinski@wsj.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 June 20, 2014 15:19 ET (19:19 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140620ea6k002l8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
