\par\par \b \uc2 Colombia May Not Act Despite Weakening Peso -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,315 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Mai 2014\par 16:28\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 10:28 EDT - The Colombian peso has been a popular trade with emerging-markets investors this year, strengthening to a 6-month high against the dollar. \b \highlight22\uc2 Colombia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has a history of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the peso to help local exporters, but \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2 's Eamon Aghdasi says that's unlikely for now. He says the fact that \uc2 Colombia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  unexpectedly hiked interest rates last week shows they're becoming more concerned about controlling inflation and less willing to maintain a weak exchange rate. For now, investors can keep piling into the peso and local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bonds without fear of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , he says. USD/COP at 1902.6 from as low as 1898 earlier in the session. (nicole.hong@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 10:11 EDT - For dollar bulls, things may get worse before they get better. The prospect of dollar strength comes from decreasing slack in the labor market, tightening monetary policy and rising rates. But "these are down the road rather than around the corner," says Citi's Steven Englander. "Before we get there we have to deal with Fed dovishness, ECB and BoJ reticence on policy ease, reserves diversification." Englander admits Citi has been hurt by overestimating how quickly dollar-strength drivers would come into play and underestimating negative near-term factors. So what's the turning point? When the ECB and BoJ pull the trigger on further easing while the Fed tightens. (cynthia.lin@wsj.com; @cynthialin_dj)\par \par \uc2 1408 GMT [Dow Jones]--Using a fair value model, TRY/ZAR is now overbought and looking at positioning it is favorable to go short, says \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2  strategist Bilal Hafeez. On the same basis, ZAR and TRY are both cheap while NZD and KRW look expensive. Elsewhere, "it may be time to revise our bearish EUR/USD forecasts. However, the case for the broader USD uptrend remains intact," he adds. (chiara.albanese@wsj.com @chiaraalbanese)\par \par \uc2 1400 GMT [Dow Jones]--The U.K. economy expanded 1.0% in the three months to the end of April and is within touching distance of its prerecession peak, says the National Institute of Economic and Social Research, a nonpartisan think tank. NIESR estimates U.K. gross domestic product was just 0.17% smaller at the end of April than it was just before the economy entered recession in early 2008, suggesting that all the ground lost in the slump will very soon be recovered. Before those champagne corks pop, NIESR reminds us this is the slowest recovery on record in the U.K. The U.S. recovered its precrisis size in 2010. (jason.douglas@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 09:50 EDT - Economists are using adjectives such as "lousy" and "ugly" to describe Canadian employment data for April, which indicated a job decline of 28,900. Desjardins Capital Markets says with the exception of job growth in Canada's largest province, Ontario, there's "nothing too positive that can be said about the report." The firm adds that the result will raise eyebrows at the \uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2 , as the 30,000-plus decline in full-time employment won't be "reassuring" in the context of labor market slack in the country. (paul.vieira@wsj.com; @paulvieira)\par \par \uc2 9:49 EDT - Jefferies' Chris Bury contends "the risk of a large and fast selloff in bonds is very remote," saying higher yields would be met with "ready buyers." He cites last Friday's reaction to the jobs report--Treasurys initially dropped on the strong job number but steadily rebounded. "Many people already expected yields to rise, and they have been short." Bury says without signs of higher inflation, the Fed is going to take its time raising rates. (min.zeng@wsj.com; @minzengwsj)\par \par \uc2 1340 GMT [Dow Jones]--The bad run for Indian industry likely continued in March with the sector's output expected to have contracted for the fifth time in six months. Industrial production, which includes output of factories, mines and utilities, likely shrunk 1.4% from a year earlier, according to the median estimate in a poll of 11 economists by The Wall Street Journal. It had shrunk 1.9% in February. "Lead indicators suggest there has been no improvement in the manufacturing sector, which has been the biggest laggard," says Gautam Singh, an economist at brokerage Spark Capital. Manufacturing output makes up about 75% of industrial production. The data will be issued at 1200 GMT Monday. (anant.kala@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 09:40 EDT - Traders say there are two factors pushing Treasury prices lower this morning. One is investors booking profit. "Bond investors need fresh triggers for another leg down in bond yields," says Dan Mulholland, head of \uc2 US Treasury\uc2  trading at \uc2 BNY Mellon Capital Markets\uc2 . He says a second factor is dealers selling bonds to lower prices in order to sell the new bonds they accrued from this week's auction to clients. The 10-year note is 5/32 lower, yielding 2.62%. (min.zeng@wsj.com; @minzengwsj)\par \par \uc2 9:35 EDT - It's not just the US where the labor-force participation rate is at levels last seen in years. The number of employed and unemployed people as a percentage of the Canadian population fell to 66.1% last month, the lowest level since November 2001, while the employment rate--or proportion of working-age Canadians working--hit 61.5%, the least in 4 years. It's all an indication Canadian employers aren't creating enough jobs to keep pace with population growth, says Erin Weir, an economist with the \uc2 United Steelworkers\uc2  union. (paul.vieira@wsj.com; @paulvieira)\par \par \uc2 Corrections & Amplifications\par \par \uc2 This article was corrected at 10:04 ET to fix paragraph that said Canada's employment rate has reached a 14-year low. It's at a 4-year low.\par \par \uc2 09:35 EDT - Canada posts a surprise decline of jobs in April, though the jobless rate remains unchanged at 6.9% as people left the workforce. The data agency notes that when measured using concepts employed by the \uc2 US government\uc2 , Canada's jobless rate was 6% in April, or down 0.2 percentage points from 12 months earlier. Economist Bill Adams from PNC says that's low enough for labor-market tightness to begin fuelling stronger wage growth in coming months. (paul.vieira@wsj.com; @paulvieira)\par \par \uc2 9:31 EDT - With Canada unexpectedly shedding about 29K jobs in April, labor trends show that the underlying employment growth is moderating, BMO's Doug Porter says. Canada's 12-month growth trend is now just up 0.8% year-over-year, about half of what the US labor sector is looking like, he notes. While that's helped to chip away at Canada's jobless rate -- currently sitting at 6.9%, the lowest since January 2009 -- that's been offset by a decline in the participation rate. Porter says the jobs data ultimately show the \uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2  will remain on hold. (david.george-cosh@wsj.com @itsdgc)\par \par \uc2 09:22 EDT - The greenback is pushing higher against the euro, yen, sterling and the Canadian dollar. But gains have come more from profit-taking than any notable improvement in US economic fundamentals. But it's not like stronger datapoints would help anyway, says Marc Chandler, global head of \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  at \uc2 Brown Brothers Harriman\uc2 . "US data is less consequential, because many people view the Fed as being on autopilot," he says. "It's less likely to bring forward market anticipation of a rate hike." USD is up 0.2% on the yen, to Y101.81, while the euro is off 0.4%, at $1.3792. The pound is off 0.4%, to $1.6865. (james.ramage@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 May 09, 2014 10:28 ET (14:28 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140509ea59002tf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
