\par\par \b \uc2 Colombia May Not Act Despite Weakening Peso -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 148 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Mai 2014\par 16:28\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 10:28 EDT - The Colombian peso has been a popular trade with emerging-markets investors this year, strengthening to a 6-month high against the dollar. \b \highlight22\uc2 Colombia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has a history of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the peso to help local exporters, but \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2 's Eamon Aghdasi says that's unlikely for now. He says the fact that \uc2 Colombia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  unexpectedly hiked interest rates last week shows they're becoming more concerned about controlling inflation and less willing to maintain a weak exchange rate. For now, investors can keep piling into the peso and local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bonds without fear of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , he says. USD/COP at 1902.6 from as low as 1898 earlier in the session. (nicole.hong@wsj.com) \par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 May 09, 2014 10:28 ET (14:28 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140509ea59002uu\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
