\par\par \b \uc2 Winter Storms Hit Christopher & Banks Traffic -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,562 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Januar 2014\par 18:41\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 12:41 EST - Dougherty & Company says a string of large winter storms has cut into Christopher & Banks (CBK) store traffic since Black Friday, causing the firm to lower its 4Q same-store sales estimate to flat from up 2%. Firm doesn't expect a big earnings hit as CBK hasn't run any special sales during the period, but says the major weekend storms in the Midwest, Mid-Atlantic and Northeast cap potential for an upside sales surprise during the holiday season. Dougherty says inventory levels are under control and key categories are selling well. CBK drops 7.7% to $7.89, though remains up 11% over the last three months. (patrick.sullivan@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 12:25 EST - Arabica-coffee futures rise as money managers buy after prices dipped to near-three-week lows earlier this week; traders expect more buying ahead. Index-tracking "funds should be coming into the market (next week) and buying up about 11,000 lots over five days" as they rebalance their positions at the start of the new year, says Beto Taguchi, senior trader at Ally Brazilian Coffee Merchants. The addition of that many positions would raise open interest by about 7.7%, but Mr. Taguchi says selling from producing countries would likely cap the rally before it can reach $1.20/lb., a key technical and psychological level. Arabica for March delivery on ICE was recently 3.6% higher at $1.1545 a pound. (alexandra.wexler@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 12:22 EST - In 2013, emerging-market bond funds saw their worst year of outflows since EPFR Global starting tracking them in 2003, with investors redeeming $31.3B. Emerging-market equity funds experienced their biggest outflows since 2011, losing $27.4B last year. But analysts say these figures are not a death knell for emerging markets. The bulk of these outflows were from retail investors, while longer-term institutional investors having been holding on or even adding holdings to emerging markets, fund managers say. \uc2 Barclays\uc2  notes that these hefty outflows "contrast with country/bottom-up data, which shows that foreign investors have not shifted their local equity and bond positions significantly in recent months. The divergence between bottom-up and EPFR fund flow data is probably because of the stronger representation of retail-type investors in the fund flows data." (erin.mccarthy@wsj.com; @eemccarthy)\par \par \uc2 11:52 EST - Colombia's peso often moves in step with the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  of its larger neighbor Brazil, but as the real tumbled this week on a stronger US dollar, the Colombian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has stayed range-bound, roughly between COP1,925 and COP1,945 per dollar. Today it's at COP1,937/USD. This may indicate \b \highlight22\uc2 Colombia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is taking advantage of its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  flexibility to reduce dollar buying -- which weakens the peso -- when it's not needed. Monetary authorities will buy "up to" $1B in US \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Jan-March, a $15M daily average if maxed out. But they are free to buy less. (dan.molinski@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 11:45 EST - Raymond James raises Finisar (FNSR) to strong buy from outperform, saying it thinks "investors do not appreciate how the company and its business have evolved." Datacom "continues to drive growth and is benefiting from strong tailwinds including copper to optical migration, increasing contributions from Web 2.0, and healthy datacenter capex," firm says. RJ also expects telecom to improve this year, "with most growth coming from improved spending in Asia offset by muted North America carrier capex." Firm admits investors "have been burned by Finisar's lumpiness in the past and many remain skittish," but it believes FNSR's business "has matured with improved customer mix and better exposure to datacom as opposed to telecom." Shares up 2.8% at $23.90. (john.shipman@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 11:45 EST - Because of the lateness of Thanksgiving this year, some holiday-related travel was going to fall into December for airlines, muting November results and giving an added boost to last month. Still, Delta's (DAL) 10% jump from a year earlier for a key revenue measure, which followed November's 3% drop, topped the company's 7-9% prediction and "was well ahead" of what S&P Capital IQ anticipated. It boosts its price target 1/3 to $40 and EPS views through 2014 as the firm upgrades DAL--the first to release December results--to strong buy from buy. "We see DAL as best in class among US airlines." Shares, which more than doubled last year to hit new highs, are atop the S&P 500 today with their 4.3% gain to $28.89. (kevin.kingsbury@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 11:12 EST - The bear of all bears, United-ICAP technician Walter Zimmerman, expects 2014 to be the year of "major reversals," with long-term time cycles suggesting stocks may be in "the bubble to end all bubbles." He says the DJIA can still rise up to about 17150 before reversing lower. Zimmerman isn't expecting a flash-like crash, as he doesn't believe DJIA will hit a long-term cycle low until 2016, somewhere in the 5770-4650 range, or 65%-72% below current levels. DJIA now up 64 at 16506. Zimmerman also expects gold to turn higher, 10-year Treasury yields to turn lower and US dollar to bottom around the same the DJIA starts reversing. (tomi.kilgore@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 11:11 EST - \uc2 Jamba Juice\uc2 's (JMBA) 3Q same-store sales fell 3.4% on factors including uncooperative weather and promotions that didn't help move the needle much. Wedbush sees no reason why trends didn't continue last quarter as it downgrades the beverage chain to neutral and drops its price target $2 to $13. That contrasts with rivals who saw solid summer sales gains as some "jumped on the fresh-squeezed raw-vegetable-juice trend ahead of Jamba" and "launched other new products such as breakfast bowls, Greek yogurt smoothies and crepes." Meanwhile, competitors are speeding up their new-store growth thanks to Robek's and Smoothie King cutting franchise fees. JMBA, for its part, is also opening up lots of new locations. Shares fall 2.2% to $12.07 and have traded sideways nearly all decade. (kevin.kingsbury@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 11:01 EST - "New Year optimism is justified this time," proclaims Capital Economics, adding that this "could be the year that the economic recovery finally kicks into a higher gear." Firm says factors that have been holding back the economy -- like the euro-zone crisis, and "the unprecedented US fiscal squeeze" -- are fading, while aspects that've boosted growth -- like a better job market, stronger housing market, and business spending -- are strengthening. Capital Economics sees the economy growing 2.5% this year, "a shade below the consensus forecast of 2.7% and the Fed's 3.0%." (john.shipman@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 10:21 EST - The snowstorm that barreled through the Northeast had led to thinner trading. In the Treasury market, trading volume as of 10am was $56B versus a 4-week average of $90B--itself anemic due to the holiday season. Some traders expect volume to pick up in the coming week. The December jobs release is due next Friday, which could influence the pace of future tapering. Treasury yields could shoot up if job growth gathers speed, but fall if the reverse occurs. The 10-year note is 3/32 lower, yielding 2.997%. (min.zeng@wsj.com; @minzengwsj)\par \par \uc2 10:17 EST - GM skunks investors with its 6% drop in December US sales, sending shares to session lows, as the company says demand was slow to start the month but gained some steam by New Year's. One good sign for GM was signs that quickly growing inventories reversed some in December. At month's end, they were 4% lower than on Nov. 30, cutting the day-supply level to a non-scary 81 from 96. Still, dealer lots have plenty of product, and GM needs to prevent running plants full blast if vehicles aren't moving terribly quickly. That's what helped get the industry into last decade's fix. GM is down 3.3% at $39.60. (kevin.kingsbury@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 10:10 EST - The direction of the new year's first day of stock trading has a knack for setting the course for the year, but \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  strategist Thomas Lee says we should instead look at the first three days' performance. "In bull market years, if equities are up after the first three days of trading, they've historically gone on to post a full year gain 80% of the time," he writes. Lee expects more "solid" gains for stocks this year, based on better global economic growth leading to stronger corporate profit growth, supportive monetary policy and "attractive relative value" vs bonds. DJIA up 54 after falling 135 yesterday. (john.shipman@wsj.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par To contact the Market Talk Editors, \par Bradley Davis, 212 416-2654, bradley.davis@dowjones.com \par Kevin Kingsbury, 212 416-2354, kevin.kingsbury@dowjones.com \par John Shipman, 212 416-2181,john.shipman@dowjones.com \par Patrick Sullivan, 212 416-2326,Patrick.sullivan@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 January 03, 2014 12:41 ET (17:41 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140103ea13001xu\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
