\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Colombia \highlight\uc2  Peso in Tight Ranges on Flexible  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 146 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Januar 2014\par 17:52\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 11:52 EST - \b \highlight22\uc2 Colombia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's peso often moves in step with the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  of its larger neighbor Brazil, but as the real tumbled this week on a stronger US dollar, the Colombian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has stayed range-bound, roughly between COP1,925 and COP1,945 per dollar. Today it's at COP1,937/USD. This may indicate \b \highlight22\uc2 Colombia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is taking advantage of its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  flexibility to reduce dollar buying -- which weakens the peso -- when it's not needed. Monetary authorities will buy "up to" $1B in US \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Jan-March, a $15M daily average if maxed out. But they are free to buy less. (dan.molinski@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 Call us at (212) 416-2181 or john.shipman@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 January 03, 2014 11:52 ET (16:52 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140103ea13001o4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
