\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Colombia \highlight\uc2  Peso Gains as  \highlight22\uc2 FX Intervention \highlight\uc2  Extended -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,475 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Dezember 2013\par 14:54\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2013, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 8:54 EST - \b \highlight22\uc2 Colombia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's peso is sticking to recent ranges, 0.3% stronger at COP1,926/USD, following the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's late-Friday announcement that it's extending a dollar-buying program aimed at weakening COP vs USD to help exporters. \uc2 Barclays\uc2  notes the bank's wording stipulates \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains an option but not an obligation; the bank said it would buy "up to" $1B in 1Q but set no minimum. This gives the bank flexibility to buy no dollars if Fed tapering and trade balance deterioration weakens COP naturally, rendering artificial moves unnecessary. \uc2 Barclays\uc2  sees COP averaging 1,945/USD next year. (dan.molinski@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 8:49 EST - Since March, \uc2 Xyratex\uc2  (XRTX) has been in a tight $10-12 range as the data-storage firm deals with slumping earnings and falling revenue. An agitated investor helped stir shares to start 2013, but they've been quiet since after a truce was called. That changes today as big disk-drive maker Seagate (STX) agrees to pay a net $294M, or $13.25/share, for XRTX, whose focus includes testing equipment as well as enterprise systems. With testing times increasing, "access to world-class test equipment becomes an increasingly strategic capability," says STX, which doesn't expect the deal to boost the bottom line for at least 2 years. STX rises 0.6% premarket. (kevin.kingsbury@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 8:47 EST - \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  sounds bullish on Nasdaq OMX (NDAQ) as it launches coverage, but rates shares neutral, noting NDAQ has already seen "meaningful appreciation" this year (up about 60% YTD at Friday's close). "We believe Nasdaq will experience multiple expansion as its revenue mix migrates toward more recurring/stable businesses," JPM says, and also sees revenue growth "being helped by the acquisitions of Thomson Reuters Solutions and eSpeed Treasury, driven by cost cutting and product development." While the stock still has some upside room vs JPM's $44.50 target, "we await a better entry point," the firm writes. (john.shipman@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 8:39 EST - Treasurys bounce off session lows as just-released mixed data suggest the Fed could take a gradual approach in tapering. November personal-spending growth met forecasts while income rose less than predicted while inflation continues to be well-contained. Meanwhile, liquidity is liable to be thin with the holidays at hand--which could exaggerate yield moves. The benchmark 10-year note is 4/32 lower, yielding 2.902%. It got to a 3-month high of 2.964% Friday before buyers stepped in. (min.zeng@wsj.com; @minzengwsj)\par \par \uc2 8:37 EST - Disinflation remains a problem, especially for Fed thinking about more tapering in the future. The latest income/spending report shows consumer prices rose 0.9% in the year ended in November. Core PCE--the Fed's preferred price measure--increased just 1.1%, well below the 2% target. A lack of pricing power will figure into Fed decision-making. (kathleen.madigan@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 8:35 EST - The tease between rivals \uc2 Jos A Bank\uc2  (JOSB) and \uc2 Men's Wearhouse\uc2  (MW) continues, with JOSB--2 months after seeing its takeover approach get rejected--today does the same to the offer MW made 4 weeks ago. Like MW did, JOSB also says the bid is far too cheap. When JOSB made its effort to buy MW public, officials admitted they weren't opposed to getting bought by MW as they ultimately thought the retailers were likely stronger together than apart. JOSB is continuing its own review of options. Maybe that includes a new run at MW. Perhaps MW will boost its offer on JOSB. Regardless, this dalliance is far from over. MW falls 1.6% premarket while JOSB is inactive. (kevin.kingsbury@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 8:27 EST - William Blair thinks Fastenal's (FAST) 4Q warning Friday "is not the result of slowing December sales trends; rather, it speaks to the lack of leverage without double-digit sales growth." As such, the investment bank believes the industrial-product distributor "is using the lackluster fourth-quarter growth as a motivational tool to inspire a real sense of urgency among employees that double-digit growth is the goal for January. If Fastenal returns to 12%-plus top-line growth early next year, we believe all will be forgiven and that the leverage will take care of itself." FAST fell 5% on the warning, a move William Blair calls "overdone" as a 1-2c "EPS miss is not evidence that the growth story is broken or that growth initiatives are not paying off." (kevin.kingsbury@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 8:19 EST - \uc2 Kinder Morgan Energy Partners\uc2  (KMP) is expanding beyond pipelines, agreeing to pay $962M for 5 cargo ships which are an average 4 years old and 4 more set to be delivered in 2015 and 2016 through buying \uc2 American Petroleum Tankers\uc2 . It's majority owned by Blackstone (BX), with fellow private-equity firm Cerberus holding the rest. APT iced its planned IPO effort last month without explanation--but now we apparently know why. The ships would only be used to transport crude and products to different points of the US, not across national borders. With shale output continuing to grow, "there is more demand for waterborne transportation," says KMP. Inactive premarket, it's trended downward most of this year after a January surge. (kevin.kingsbury@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 7:58 EST - Since Morsi's removal as Egyptian president in early July, the country's economy has begun to make a comeback, says Capital Economics. Egypt's PMI hit a record 52.5 for November as a $4.5B aid package helped Egypt run a current-account surplus in 3Q for the first time since 2009. As a result, Egypt's stock index is rebounding, rising 23% for the year, while the pound has stabilized at EGP6.9/dollar and spreads have narrowed by more than 3 percentage points on its dollar bonds. Capital Economics expects "progress on the political front" which could lead to elections next year. (prabrha.natarajan@wsj.com; @prabhanatarjan)\par \par \uc2 7:35 EST - Irrigation-system provider Lindsay (LNN) ended a streak of strong upside surprises with a noted shortfall in F4Q's results in October. When 1Q's numbers are released on Jan. 3, that print could begin "a downward-revision cycle," predicts Janney. That as the \uc2 Kansas City Fed\uc2  recently noted in its quarterly report on agriculture-credit conditions that farm incomes fell in 3Q amid falling crop prices and were likely to have stayed "soft" this quarter "despite some support from crop insurance and a gradual improvement in livestock-sector profitability." Meanwhile, the KC Fed's index of farm operating loan demand hit a 5-year high in 3Q in the wake of "reduced farm income and high production costs." LNN has risen just 2% this year. (kevin.kingsbury@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 7:30 EST - Generous spirit of the holidays and a firm belief in Santa help maintain the bullish bias for US stocks. A glance around your train, bus or roadway this morning confirms the obvious -- with the midweek holiday, many market participants are sidelined, which tends to contribute to choppy (but often positive) trading in light volume. That's likely to be the setup until we officially hit 2014. November personal income & spending, and \uc2 Chicago Fed\uc2 's national activity index due at 8:30am ET; Reuters/Univ of Michigan final December consumer sentiment out at 9:55am. S&P futures up 7.00; 10-yr note lower yield at 2.90%. (john.shipman@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 7:15 EST - Joe Eazor, who headed H-P's (HPQ) enterprise-services for a time after the company's 2008 acquisition of EDS before becoming a consultant with McKinsey and then an executive at client EMC, will be the next CEO of Internet-service provider EarthLink (ELNK). The company has been moving to become more of a tech-services provider, and 3/4 of revenue now comes from non-consumer channels. "EarthLink customers and investors will benefit from Joe's substantial sales, strategy and \b \highlight22\uc2 operational\b0 \highlight\uc2  experience in some of the largest leading-edge technology companies in the world," says current CEO Rolla Huff, who will remain chairman for a time. ELNK shares have been on a 3-year downslide amid its transition, losing nearly half their value to reach 10-year lows. They're inactive premarket. (kevin.kingsbury@wsj.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par To contact the Market Talk Editors, \par Bradley Davis, 212 416-2654, bradley.davis@dowjones.com \par Kevin Kingsbury, 212 416-2354, kevin.kingsbury@dowjones.com \par John Shipman, 212 416-2181,john.shipman@dowjones.com \par Patrick Sullivan, 212 416-2326,Patrick.sullivan@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 December 23, 2013 08:54 ET (13:54 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020131223e9cn001e6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
