\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Colombia \highlight\uc2  Peso Gains as  \highlight22\uc2 FX Intervention \highlight\uc2  Extended -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,251 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Dezember 2013\par 14:54\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2013, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 8:54 EST - \b \highlight22\uc2 Colombia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's peso is sticking to recent ranges, 0.3% stronger at COP1,926/USD, following the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's late-Friday announcement that it's extending a dollar-buying program aimed at weakening COP vs USD to help exporters. \uc2 Barclays\uc2  notes the bank's wording stipulates \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains an option but not an obligation; the bank said it would buy "up to" $1B in 1Q but set no minimum. This gives the bank flexibility to buy no dollars if Fed tapering and trade balance deterioration weakens COP naturally, rendering artificial moves unnecessary. \uc2 Barclays\uc2  sees COP averaging 1,945/USD next year. (dan.molinski@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 Call us at (212) 416-2181 or john.shipman@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 8:39 EST - Treasurys bounce off session lows as just-released mixed data suggest the Fed could take a gradual approach in tapering. November personal-spending growth met forecasts while income rose less than predicted while inflation continues to be well-contained. Meanwhile, liquidity is liable to be thin with the holidays at hand--which could exaggerate yield moves. The benchmark 10-year note is 4/32 lower, yielding 2.902%. It got to a 3-month high of 2.964% Friday before buyers stepped in. (min.zeng@wsj.com; @minzengwsj)\par \par \uc2 8:37 EST - Disinflation remains a problem, especially for Fed thinking about more tapering in the future. The latest income/spending report shows consumer prices rose 0.9% in the year ended in November. Core PCE--the Fed's preferred price measure--increased just 1.1%, well below the 2% target. A lack of pricing power will figure into Fed decision-making. (kathleen.madigan@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1332 GMT [Dow Jones] Euro Overnight Index Average fixed higher Friday at 0.161% versus Thursday's 0.159%. Monday, the interbank overnight rate is trading unchanged between 0.08% and 0.22%. Euro-zone banks received EUR133.58 billion from the ECB at this week's main refinancing \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2 , up from EUR118.91 last week. "We can see that banks are paying back cash from the long-term tenders and are using ECB money to make it through the year-end," one trader explains. Earlier Monday, 3-month Euribor fixed at 0.294% vs 0.292% Friday. (thomas.leppert@dowjones.com) Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 Markettalk.eu@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 1316 GMT [Dow Jones] With USD/CAD's MACD in sell territory, the 9-day moving average flirting with a break below the 21-day, and the candlestick pattern highlighting strong downside momentum, it's no real surprise that Scotiabank's bias is on the downside. Now at 1.0600, the bank has a target for its short trade of the 100DMA at 1.0440. gary.stride@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 1315 GMT [Dow Jones]--The euro zone will have the advantage of base effects to ensure that 2014's GDP readings appear, statistically at least, as though there's improvement, says \uc2 UBS\uc2  strategist Simon Penn. "But it will be illusionary," he goes on, and, by the middle of next year, "growth will be shown to be lacking." Mr. Penn worries that bank lending data will have again deteriorated, while PMI data are likely to be rolling over. Upshot: resting on the relative euro-zone calm of the last year could be dangerous. "Europe might not go back to full blown crisis in 2014, but certainly the markets will come to see that the economic improvements do not match with those built elsewhere." (david.cottle@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 7:58 EST - Since Morsi's removal as Egyptian president in early July, the country's economy has begun to make a comeback, says Capital Economics. Egypt's PMI hit a record 52.5 for November as a $4.5B aid package helped Egypt run a current-account surplus in 3Q for the first time since 2009. As a result, Egypt's stock index is rebounding, rising 23% for the year, while the pound has stabilized at EGP6.9/dollar and spreads have narrowed by more than 3 percentage points on its dollar bonds. Capital Economics expects "progress on the political front" which could lead to elections next year. (prabrha.natarajan@wsj.com; @prabhanatarjan)\par \par \uc2 1232 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/CAD slips to a fresh one week low of 1.0596 as the big dollar comes under a touch of selling pressure into the US market open. There's data risk up ahead for the Loonie with October GDP (1330 GMT), where the DJN survey forecast is for a 0.3% month-month gain, although both \uc2 HSBC\uc2  and \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2  expect a +0.2% reading. Next chart support for USD/CAD is 1.0560, resistance at 1.0610. gary.stride@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 1229 GMT [Dow Jones]--When it comes to tighter US monetary policy, whether it be via tapering or old-fashioned rate hikes, Standard Bank's worry is that many countries around the world have imported the Fed's former, super-loose stance through their own fixed (or fixed-ish) exchange rate systems. This truth might leave the highest vulnerability to tightening in Asia, home of most such regimes. The obvious pressure points are in property, in places such as Hong Kong and Singapore, Standard says, but there's probably also a lot of susceptibility that we cannot fully see, such as corporate credit risks in China. QE may yet prove far easier to get into than to leave. (david.cottle@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1136 GMT [Dow Jones]--Every year in the markets throws up a term which moves into the mainstream from some obscure corner of the financial world. Remember when we all thought 'quantitative easing' exotic beyond words. There surely can't be much serious argument that 'tapering' was the one in 2013, although 'forward guidance' and 'shutdown' ran it close for a while there. SocGen's Kit Juckes thinks the phrase we'll all be tired of this time next year will be 'macro-prudential.' - referring to the endless post-crisis stress testing and health checking of financial institutions. Make a note. (david.cottle@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1128 GMT xx-DJ Liffe white sugar futures for March delivery trade flat at $445.90/tonne. Singapore-based Phillip Futures brokerage says that the interim rally in sugar prices at the end of last week may help to boost farmer selling in Brazil, in particular if the Brazilian real remains weak, thus potentially pressuring the sugar market lower. "Whilst this corrective rally was not unexpected, given the long downtrend seen recently and the technically oversold condition of the market, we feel it is merely a correction," adds Sucden Financial. Write to Neena Rai at neena.rai@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 1111 GMT [Dow Jones]--2013 has been the markets' best since the financial crisis hit by any number of measures, but the ghosts of Christmases past still aren't at peace by any means. Just 3% of financial-service professionals polled in a survey commissioned by global professional services firm Kinetic Partners believe the regulatory changes implemented since 2008 have done enough to prevent a future crash. Furthermore, just 12% of respondents believe that regulators fully understand how the financial crisis was allowed to happen in the first place. Happy holidays, one and all! (david.cottle@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 December 23, 2013 08:54 ET (13:54 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020131223e9cn001cc\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
