\par\par \b \uc2 Traders Increased Euro Shorts Last Week -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,454 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Dezember 2013\par 22:20\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2013, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 16:20 EST - Traders increased their anti-euro bets into Dec 17, data from the CFTC showed Friday, indicating that market players likely believe the $1.37 level is the top of the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's range. Targets for the euro may drift lower, however, now that the Fed has announced its long-awaited taper and breathed new life into the dollar, analysts said. "The euro has surrendered gains that have come from more seasonal factors such as end of year repatriation from area banks," says Joe Manimbo, market analyst at \uc2 Western Union\uc2 . "Once the euro's seasonal support fades away, the euro could be vulnerable early in the new year." EUR/USD recently traded at $1.3677, up 0.1%. (ira.iosebashvili@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 Call us at (212) 416-2181 or john.shipman@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 15:09 EST - Today's trading mix in Treasurys shows clear selling in front-end notes and buying in long-dated maturities. While it's just one session, it's not the direction the Fed wants to see yields travel. The Fed's shift away from QE to the promise of low rates is intended to pin down short-end rates and allow long-end yields to drift up in line with a stronger economy. "It does not appear that investors are buying into the Fed's current guidance," Citi writes, wondering if the Fed is losing control of its ability to impact yields. Three-year notes down 3/32 to yield 0.73% and 10s up 11/32 to yield 2.885%. (cynthia.lin@wsj.com; @cynthialin_dj)\par \par \uc2 Call us at (212) 416-2181 or john.shipman@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 14:29 EST - In accordance with Bernanke's guidance and \uc2 Barclays\uc2 ' own forecast of "moderate real GDP growth, a continued brisk rate of decline in the unemployment rate, and gradually rising core inflation," the firm sees the Fed trimming purchases by $10B at each meeting through September, with a final $15B cut at the Oct 28-29 meeting. \uc2 Barclays\uc2  previously expected a March-to-September wrap period, but this longer, slower timeframe gives the Fed more time to assess incoming economic data, and any ill effects from tapering. Firm still sees first rate hike in June 2015, with fed funds at 1% two years from now. (john.shipman@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 Call us at (212) 416-2181 or john.shipman@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 14:27 EST - With persistent dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Colombia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the peso has fallen 10% this year, wiping away all of 2012's gains. But the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which does the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to help Colombian exporters, isn't satisfied yet. Bank President Uribe said today it'll keep \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the 1Q of 2014, to the tune of "up to" $1B, because he said the bank wants the peso even weaker. That could work out to about $15M a day, roughly equal to its current purchases. COP ended today at 1,934/USD. (dan.molinski@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 12:38 EST - Drummond has had a rough year in Colombia, but the Birmingham-based mining company says it's wiping off its soot-covered face, ready for 2014. Colombia yesterday fined Drummond $3.6M for a January environmental incident where it dumped hundreds of tons of coal into the Caribbean during choppy seas. The president of Drummond, present in Colombia since the 1990s, said today he "laments" the incident that halted exports for weeks and said it, along with labor woes, are to blame for output falling to 22M tons this year from 25M in 2012. (dan.molinski@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 12:38 EST - In an unexpected move, many emerging-market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are making a modest comeback. The South African rand is seeing some buyers after the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  almost hit the critical ZAR10.5/dollar earlier Friday, says John Wood of Standard Chartered. The greenback is now weaker on the day at ZAR10.3340. Meanwhile, there's "dominant buying interest" for Mexican and Colombian bonds, says Eduardo Suarez of Scotiabank. Alongside that, the peso has wiped out its earlier weakness and is up 0.1% against the dollar at MXN12.9540. "We expect buyers to slowly come back to EM in the next couple of weeks," says Wood. For the past two sessions, market participants have favored the dollar versus EM \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . (prabha.natarajan@wsj.com; @prabhanatarjan)\par \par \uc2 12:16 EST - If history is any guidance, the end of Fed bond-buying programs means a selloff, followed by a bigger rebound as stocks stumble on the lack of monetary support. This has burned many a bond bear the past few years and why bears should gauge how equities fare in the current taper world before jumping into shorts. Indeed, 10-year notes have reversed early weakness and are up 5/32, yielding 2.905%. But a case can be made that history is no longer a good guide. The economic recovery, however slow, seems to have investors confident enough to send stocks to new highs. (cynthia.lin@wsj.com; @cynthialin_dj)\par \par \uc2 11:14 EST - \uc2 Laurentian Bank Securities\uc2  says the \uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2  is growing "increasingly" uncomfortable with the state of tepid inflation. Based on November data, the all-items CPI advanced just 0.9%. That's the second straight month of a less-than-1% climb in prices, and the 19th consecutive month inflation was below the BOC's 2% target. Odds of the BOC turning even more dovish at its next rate decision on Jan. 22 "have increased significantly," firm said. "We are of the view that the below-desired inflation malaise is deepening," and rate cuts "are on the verge of becoming part of the base-case scenario for 2014." (paul.vieira@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 10:13 EST - Anxieties about Ukraine's finances and a potential \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  crisis has calmed significantly since Russia lent a helping hand. Exotix says the recent rally in Ukraine sovereign bonds is justified and maintains a buy recommendation as the government's liquidity constraints ease. But to monitor any potential run on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the firm created a Ukraine stress index, combining measures of the nation's balance of payments, domestic interest rates, equity prices and external funding costs. The index, now at 0.6, is far below the 10.3 hit during a funding crisis in late-2008. (cynthia.lin@wsj.com; @cynthialin_dj)\par \par \uc2 09:28 EST - Bond prices bouncing back once again just as the 10-year yield crept up close to the 3% mark. The yield earlier hit a three-month peak of 2.964% right after the GDP report, but has since fallen back. Buyers again show up to grab at a yield near 3%. Among these buyers has been Japanese investors lately. The benchmark 10-year note is now 5/32 higher, yielding 2.905%. (min.zeng@wsj.com; @minzengwsj)\par \par \uc2 9:25 EST - The long bond has been a winner amid selling in Treasurys, getting key support from unwinding of steepening-yield-curve trades. Investors in recent weeks have piled into buying 5s and selling 10s and 30s, betting that tapering is not tightening. Yet it appears some have doubts about the Fed's commitment to keeping rates lower for longer. That shift has generated big selloff in 5s the past 2 sessions while boosting the 30-year bond price. "The tapering cycle has finally begun and Yellen seems to be on board with Bernanke, so there is no reason to think she will hit the brakes once she takes over as long as the data continues to improve," says Jason Rogan at Guggenheim Securities. 5s are down 6/32 today while 30s rise 16/32. (min.zeng@wsj.com, @minzengwsj)\par \par \uc2 09:24 EST - Pressure on middle-dated Treasurys shows how Yellen might face an uphill battle soothing investors about interest rates. Friday's strong revision of 3Q GDP adds to bondholders' anxiety that if economic growth accelerates, the Fed might speed up tapering and bring forward the timing of its first rate hike. "It is a reflection that the market seems to believe that Fed will have a difficult time holding zero interest policy in place if the economy is in fact improving," says Larry Milstein at R.W. Pressprich. Still, the pace of the rise in yields has been moderate compared to the May-June selloff. Buying interest near 3% has tempered the rise, and ten-year notes recently swung positive, up 5/32, yielding 2.909%. (min.zeng@wsj.com; @minzengwsj)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 December 20, 2013 16:20 ET (21:20 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020131220e9ck003ie\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
