\par\par \b \uc2 Barclays\uc2  Sees Fed Bond Buying Ending in Oct 2014 -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,495 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Dezember 2013\par 20:29\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2013, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 14:29 EST - In accordance with Bernanke's guidance and \uc2 Barclays\uc2 ' own forecast of "moderate real GDP growth, a continued brisk rate of decline in the unemployment rate, and gradually rising core inflation," the firm sees the Fed trimming purchases by $10B at each meeting through September, with a final $15B cut at the Oct 28-29 meeting. \uc2 Barclays\uc2  previously expected a March-to-September wrap period, but this longer, slower timeframe gives the Fed more time to assess incoming economic data, and any ill effects from tapering. Firm still sees first rate hike in June 2015, with fed funds at 1% two years from now. (john.shipman@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 14:27 EST - With persistent dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Colombia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the peso has fallen 10% this year, wiping away all of 2012's gains. But the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which does the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to help Colombian exporters, isn't satisfied yet. Bank President Uribe said today it'll keep \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the 1Q of 2014, to the tune of "up to" $1B, because he said the bank wants the peso even weaker. That could work out to about $15M a day, roughly equal to its current purchases. COP ended today at 1,934/USD. (dan.molinski@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 13:33 EST - \uc2 Nike\uc2  (NKE) shares down 1.3% at $77.27 and currently the biggest drag on a rallying DJIA. Canaccord says F2Q was "a fine quarter with no surprises," but "as we expected expenses are accelerating resulting in guidance that implies a meaningful reduction to Q3 and Q4 estimates." The firm also says "gross margin headwinds are mounting as the favorability from raw materials has turned and supply chain disruptions in Mexico will persist for two [quarters]." Given NKE's "premium 25x multiple," Canaccord says, "we prefer to play its strength through its retail partners," \uc2 Foot Locker\uc2  (FL) and Finish Line (FINL). FL up 7%, FINL rises 2.4%. (john.shipman@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 13:11 EST - Sterne Agee doesn't see much to spur on bank stocks entering 2014, "as earnings expectations slow dramatically to a mid single-digit pace." Near-term, the firm thinks "owning controversial names at this stage represents the best opportunity to outperform," while it wants to avoid banks that "have only modest earnings leverage, relatively full valuations, excess mortgage exposure absent servicing offset, and are vulnerable to still-subdued loan growth." Sterne Agee sees most favorable risk/reward in \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  (JPM) and Citi (C), which trade at "significant discounts to the broader group," and should benefit from better economy in 2014. M&T's (MTB) a top regional pick, "owing primarily to outsized expectations for acquisition-levered earnings growth." (john.shipman@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 12:45 EST - \uc2 Altria\uc2  (MO) getting entre into the global e-cigarette business and not being limited to the US market has shares at a fresh record and in closer ties with a company it spun off in 2008. Licensing its offerings to Philip Morris (PM) for overseas distribution "could have a significant positive financial impact, particularly for MO," says \uc2 Wells Fargo\uc2 . It also thinks the companies tying up "gives them a competitive advantage--stronger together than alone" as the industry continues to develop and is poised to continue its meteoric growth. "We continue to believe consumption of e-cigs could surpass consumption of traditional cigs in the next decade in the US." MO is up 0.7% at $38.48 while PM is flat. (kevin.kingsbury@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 12:38 EST - Drummond has had a rough year in Colombia, but the Birmingham-based mining company says it's wiping off its soot-covered face, ready for 2014. Colombia yesterday fined Drummond $3.6M for a January environmental incident where it dumped hundreds of tons of coal into the Caribbean during choppy seas. The president of Drummond, present in Colombia since the 1990s, said today he "laments" the incident that halted exports for weeks and said it, along with labor woes, are to blame for output falling to 22M tons this year from 25M in 2012. (dan.molinski@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 12:38 EST - In an unexpected move, many emerging-market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are making a modest comeback. The South African rand is seeing some buyers after the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  almost hit the critical ZAR10.5/dollar earlier Friday, says John Wood of Standard Chartered. The greenback is now weaker on the day at ZAR10.3340. Meanwhile, there's "dominant buying interest" for Mexican and Colombian bonds, says Eduardo Suarez of Scotiabank. Alongside that, the peso has wiped out its earlier weakness and is up 0.1% against the dollar at MXN12.9540. "We expect buyers to slowly come back to EM in the next couple of weeks," says Wood. For the past two sessions, market participants have favored the dollar versus EM \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . (prabha.natarajan@wsj.com; @prabhanatarjan)\par \par \uc2 12:29 EST - \uc2 Boston Fed\uc2  chief Rosengren explains why he dissented at Wednesday's FOMC announcement to start pulling back on stimulus. He doesn't disagree that the economy has made enough progress. The difference for him is that he doesn't have "sufficient confidence" in the improved outlook. "My decision to cast a dissenting vote was focused on counseling patience in removing monetary accommodation," he writes. And because the Fed is falling short on both its full-employment and inflation mandates, he said he felt changing the program was "premature." (cynthia.lin@wsj.com; @cynthialin_dj)\par \par \uc2 12:16 EST - If history is any guidance, the end of Fed bond-buying programs means a selloff, followed by a bigger rebound as stocks stumble on the lack of monetary support. This has burned many a bond bear the past few years and why bears should gauge how equities fare in the current taper world before jumping into shorts. Indeed, 10-year notes have reversed early weakness and are up 5/32, yielding 2.905%. But a case can be made that history is no longer a good guide. The economic recovery, however slow, seems to have investors confident enough to send stocks to new highs. (cynthia.lin@wsj.com; @cynthialin_dj)\par \par \uc2 12:16 EST - In a CNBC interview, \uc2 BlackBerry\uc2  (BBRY) interim CEO \uc2 John Chen\uc2  was quick to highlight the company's key strengths as it attempts to return to profitability sometime in fiscal 2016. Among its strong support in the enterprise sector, Chen emphasized the device remains the most secure product on the market, name-dropping the German chancellor, and officials from the White House and NATO as customers who haven't given up on the brand just yet. Chen's efforts to change BBRY to a leaner, more-focused company are being welcomed by investors. Shares up almost 12% at $6.97, but still off 20% in the past three months. (david.george-cosh@wsj.com @itsdgc)\par \par \uc2 12:14 EST - In the wake of Carnival's (CCL) better-than-feared F4Q results and decent guidance, \uc2 UBS\uc2  upgrades the cruise operator and rival Royal Caribbean (RCL) to buy. On CCL, "we are willing to pay for 2015 today now that we are seeing very visible signs of recover, and thus we are removing the [roughly] 15% discount" in prior estimates. \uc2 UBS\uc2  boosts its price target to $41 from $34, while on RCL it climbs $5 to $50 as it sees the company benefiting from CCL's "holding-price" strategy of not slashing fares on soon-to-depart vessels. The industry bigwig not being as promotional "can significantly affect the overall price environment." CCL, after rising 2.5% Thursday, gains another 1.4% to $38.59 while RCL climbs 1.2% to $46.45 in hitting a 2 1/2-year high. (kevin.kingsbury@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 12:00 EST - \uc2 Zumiez\uc2  (ZUMZ) shares up 9% at $26.94, helped by a BofA/Merrill upgrade to buy from neutral and an ensuing spell of short covering. BofA offers the standard "well positioned for long-term growth" bromide, while noting a recent selloff has taken ZUMZ's valuation to "near historical lows" and below peers. "We think the current valuation discount is unwarranted as we expect \uc2 Zumiez\uc2  to grow earnings faster than its specialty retail peers (13.5% over 2014-2015 versus 11.5% for the group) through consistent new store expansion and ecommerce growth," BofA says. Firm sees near-term EPS upside potential, with checks indicating "discounting is under control and favorable calendar shifts should provide an incremental boost to December comps." (john.shipman@wsj.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par To contact the Market Talk Editors, \par Bradley Davis, 212 416-2654, bradley.davis@dowjones.com \par Kevin Kingsbury, 212 416-2354, kevin.kingsbury@dowjones.com \par John Shipman, 212 416-2181,john.shipman@dowjones.com \par Patrick Sullivan, 212 416-2326,Patrick.sullivan@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 December 20, 2013 14:29 ET (19:29 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020131220e9ck00339\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
