\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Colombia \highlight\uc2  Peso Falls Even with Reduced  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,639 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Oktober 2013\par 17:13\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global Equities News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 11:13 EDT - Broad gains today in the dollar pushed Colombia's peso to its weakest since Oct. 1 at 1896.3/USD, good news for \uc2 Colombia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  which this month dialed back its costly \b \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  program. The bank was buying $30M daily in the spot market to keep the peso weak against the greenback and help Colombian exporters, but will now only average $15B in daily dollar purchases from October through December. Some fear reduced \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will lead to strong peso gains that again put exporters at a disadvantage, but so far COP remains near authorities' "comfort" range of 1,900-1,950 per dollar. (dan.molinski@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 11:05 EDT - \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2  has spoken a number of times about the way the jobs market is progressing, placing a focus beyond a declining unemployment rate. Expecting Yellen's bar on an improved labor market to be a bit higher, Capital Economics says, "There is a slightly bigger risk...the Fed will fail to tighten monetary policy in time once the recovery gathers momentum" under her leadership, "eventually triggering an unwanted surge in inflation." That could be behind today's notable lag in long-end Treasurys, with 30-year bonds down 17/32, yielding 3.724%. Ten-year inflation-protected Treasurys are outperforming, reflecting 2.21% in expected inflation, versus 2.19% yesterday. (cynthia.lin@wsj.com; @cynthialin_dj)\par \par \uc2 1505 GMT xxxx GMT DJ Amid low-volume trade, the Polish government debt curve flattened slightly as the market awaits another deadline in the U.S. budget and debt row. Average yields on 2-year bond rose to 3.25% from 3.14% on late Tuesday, the 5-year is down to 3.73% from 3.75% and yield on the 10-year is at 4.38% from 4.41%. (patryk.wasilewski@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 10:39 EDT - Among the hard-hit T-bills due this month and early November, the batch that the \uc2 US Treasury\uc2  sold Tuesday faces the biggest risk of default, some traders say. While the Treasury puts Oct. 17 as the estimated deadline for a debt-ceiling deal, the US still has cash to pay interest on T-bills, notes and bonds through Oct. 30, many traders and economists estimate. They believe a deal will emerge to avert default but that it could come after Oct. 17. In that scenario, T-bills due Nov. 7 are the most likely to see delayed interest payment. For now, the yield on T-bills due November 7 is 0.28%, lower than the 0.43% yield on T-bills due Oct. 17. (min.zeng@wsj.com; @minzengwsj)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ) Here are details of this week's remaining government bond auctions in Europe:\par \par \uc2 Thursday, October 10\par \par \uc2 0900 GMT Italy: EUR8.5B 12-month T-bills\par \par \uc2 0930 GMT Hungary: HUF50B 12-month T-bills\par \par \uc2 1200 GMT Hungary: target volume for 3-month T-bill auction on Oct 15\par \par \uc2 1400 GMT Belgium: info on ORI auction on Oct 11\par \par \uc2 Xxxx Portugal: final terms of T-bill auction on Oct 16\par \par \uc2 Friday, October 11\par \par \uc2 0900 GMT Italy: EUR3B-EUR3.5B 2.75% November 2016 BTP, EUR750M-EUR1.25B 4.75% September 2028 BTP, EUR750M-EUR1.25B November 2018 CCTeu\par \par \uc2 0900 GMT France: details of BTAN, linker auction on Oct 17\par \par \uc2 0900 GMT France: details of T-bill auction on Oct 14\par \par \uc2 1000 GMT Belgium: ORI auction (according to Thursday's announcement)\par \par \uc2 1200 GMT Spain: details of bond auction on Oct 17\par \par \uc2 Xxxx Belgium: target volume for Treasury certificate auction on Oct 15\par \par \uc2 Xxxx Weekly EMU bond supply preview\par \par \uc2 (christopher.tomkinson@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0000 GMT (\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 )-- Here are details of the remaining government bond auctions this week:\par \par \uc2 Thursday, October 10\par \par \uc2 0900 GMT Italy: EUR8.5B 12-month T-bills\par \par \uc2 0930 GMT Hungary: HUF50B 12-month T-bills\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    1200 GMT Hungary: target volume for 3-month T-bill                         auction on Oct 15 \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1400 GMT Belgium: info on ORI auction on Oct 11 Xxxx Portugal: final terms of 16/10 T-bill auction\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Friday, October 11\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0900 GMT Italy: EUR3B-EUR3.5B 2.75% November 2016 BTP, EUR750M-EUR1.25B 4.75% September 2028 BTP, EUR750M-EUR1.25B November 2018 CCTeu\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    0900 GMT France: details of BTAN, linker auction on                                        Oct 17 \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0900 GMT France: details of T-bill auction on Oct 14\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1000 GMT Belgium: ORI auction (according to Thursday's announcement)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1200 GMT Spain: details of bond auction on Oct 17\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Xxxx Belgium: target volume for Treasury certificate auction on Oct 15\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Xxxx Weekly EMU bond supply preview\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (christopher.tomkinson@wsj.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 10:16 EDT - T-bills due this month to about Nov. 14 continue to get hit, with the yield on Oct. 17 T-bills yielding above 0.4%. But maturities beyond that remain sharply lower. That's a sign investors don't want to deal with potential \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operational\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  headaches if the \f28 \uc2 \f2 US government\f28 \uc2 \f2  delays payments, but still believe any missed payments would be short-lived. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Thomas Urano\f28 \uc2 \f2 , money-market portfolio manager at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Sage Advisory Services\f28 \uc2 \f2 , says he still holds some T-bills due Nov. 29. "If there is sign of a further rise in T-bill yields and the selling spills over to other maturities, I would think about cashing out my T-bills due Nov. 29 and put cash in T-bills in maturities later than that," he says, though he doesn't believe the US will default. (min.zeng@wsj.com; @minzengwsj)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0000 GMT (\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 )-- U.K.'s August industrial production (fell 1.1%, well below expectation, with manufacturing production falling 1.2%) should be seen as a statistical outlier, says Daniel Vernazza, an economist with \f28 \uc2 \f2 UniCredit\f28 \uc2 \f2  Research in London.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 He points out that the month-on-month number is very volatile, and suggests the 3-month moving average index as a better guide to the true state of affairs. The index of industrial production rose to 96.5 in August from 96.3 in July (+0.2% mom), while the index of manufacturing rose to 100.3 in August from 100.0 in July (+0.3% mom). Since last November, the trend in industrial production has been clearly positive.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Mr Vernazza also draws attention to the fact that changes in industrial production tend to lag movements in the manufacturing PMI by around 4 months. In April (4 months prior to August), the manufacturing PMI reading was just 50.6. Since then the PMI has accelerated sharply, to 57.1 in August, before easing marginally to 56.7 in September.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In summary therefore, he says: "Given the volatility of the industrial production series and the upbeat sentiment figures, we expect a strong bounce next month. We stick to our GDP growth forecast of 0.9% qoq in 3Q13, and annual growth of 1.6% in 2013 and 3.1% in 2014, which is well above consensus."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (christopher.tomkinson@wsj.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 09:59 EDT - \f28 \uc2 \f2 Russ Koesterich\f28 \uc2 \f2 , BlackRock's global chief investment strategist, believes the Fed under Yellen's leadership likely means no rate hike before 2015. He says implications for policy outlook are twofold. While the Fed could still taper QE3 as soon as December, the pace will be slow and dependent on the US economy. Also, "a Yellen-run Fed would likely place significant weight on the 2nd part of the Fed's dual mandate -- full employment -- even at the cost of a temporary rise in inflation." News that President Obama will nominate Yellen is boosting shorter-dated Treasurys, where yields are sensitive to the policy-rate outlook. Two-year notes up 1/32, yielding 0.369%. (min.zeng@wsj.com; @minzengwsj)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 9:54 EDT - A polarized Congress and drawn-out budget debate could eat away at investors' long-term outlook on Treasurys. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Goldman Sachs\f28 \uc2 \f2  says that would build up a "fiscal premium" demanded on longer maturities, not necessarily reflecting serious default concerns, but fears that political standoffs in the US will happen more frequently and risk mismanaging inflation. In that vein, Goldman recommends betting on a steeper yield curve: a lower 3-year yield as growth concerns increase and higher 30-year yield on that fiscal concern. The 3- to 30-year yield gap is currently 303bps; Goldman targets 340bps. (cynthia.lin@wsj.com; @cynthialin_dj)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 9:40 EDT - Venezuela hasn't made needed changes yet to its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  system because of internal government debate and policy paralysis ahead of municipal elections in December, contends Eurasia. There's a rising perception that Venezuela risks an economic and social crisis if soaring inflation and runaway depreciation on the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  black market aren't addressed. Officials have promised a complementary \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  mechanism, and there had been hope they would introduce more flexibility to strict \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  controls. But an earlier devaluation likely hurt President Nicolas Maduro during his run earlier this year, and he can't risk a repeat in December, says Eurasia. (ezequiel.minaya@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 09:39 EDT - The US is no Greece or Argentina, so concerns about a US default isn't hammering Treasurys. Instead, investors are simply cashing out of one-month T-bills and rolling money into later maturities. That reflects Treasurys' haven status and faith that a fiscal deal will eventually emerge. Some traders say demand for this afternoon's $21B sale of 10-year notes will be decent given this backdrop. "Markets globally are concerned about the recessionary effect of a possible default, and this should help support today's auction," says Anthony Cronin, Treasury bond trader at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Soci\u233\'00\'E9t\u233\'00\'E9 G\u233\'00\'E9n\u233\'00\'E9rale\f28 \uc2 \f2 . The when-issued 10-year recently yielded 2.643%, compared to 2.946% offered at last month's 10-year sale. (min.zeng@wsj.com; @minzengwsj) [ 09-10-13 1513GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020131009e9a9000h0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
