\par\par \b \uc2 DJ  \highlight22\uc2 Colombia \highlight\uc2  Peso Falls Even with Reduced  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,482 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Oktober 2013\par 17:13\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2013, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 11:13 EDT - Broad gains today in the dollar pushed Colombia's peso to its weakest since Oct. 1 at 1896.3/USD, good news for \uc2 Colombia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  which this month dialed back its costly \b \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  program. The bank was buying $30M daily in the spot market to keep the peso weak against the greenback and help Colombian exporters, but will now only average $15B in daily dollar purchases from October through December. Some fear reduced \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will lead to strong peso gains that again put exporters at a disadvantage, but so far COP remains near authorities' "comfort" range of 1,900-1,950 per dollar. (dan.molinski@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 11:05 EDT - Janet Yellen has spoken a number of times about the way the jobs market is progressing, placing a focus beyond a declining unemployment rate. Expecting Yellen's bar on an improved labor market to be a bit higher, Capital Economics says, "There is a slightly bigger risk...the Fed will fail to tighten monetary policy in time once the recovery gathers momentum" under her leadership, "eventually triggering an unwanted surge in inflation." That could be behind today's notable lag in long-end Treasurys, with 30-year bonds down 17/32, yielding 3.724%. Ten-year inflation-protected Treasurys are outperforming, reflecting 2.21% in expected inflation, versus 2.19% yesterday. (cynthia.lin@wsj.com; @cynthialin_dj)\par \par \uc2 10:51 EDT - The broad market has stabilized but the high-profile, high-momentum stocks and ETFs are getting creamed for the second day in a row. Global X Social Media Index (SOCL), with top holdings in the likes of \uc2 Facebook\uc2  (FB), SINA, LinkedIn (LNKD) and Pandora (P) hit for 1.8% even as the S&P 500 trades modestly lower. SOCL fell 4.4% yesterday. Already this week, the ETF has lost more than 8%, a hefty chunk of the year's 43% advance. Traders say it's a lot of short-term traders locking in profits on big-winning stocks as uncertainty reigns in Washington. (christopher.dieterich@wsj.com; @chrisdieterich)\par \par \uc2 10:41 EDT - Industrial supplier Fastenal's (FAST) 3Q earnings miss "was primarily related to gross margins reported at 51.7%, below consensus at 52.3%," Wunderlich says, which follows 52.3% gross margins in 1H. "Management cited unfavorable product mix, competitive pressure and a focus on shorter cycle sales efforts all impacting profitability in Q3," firm says. Wunderlich also says FAST "significantly shifted its 'tone' regarding gross margins from one of optimism surrounding its new pricing system to greater caution related to prevailing market conditions." FAST down 4.7% at $47.65. (john.shipman@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 10:39 EDT - Among the hard-hit T-bills due this month and early November, the batch that the \uc2 US Treasury\uc2  sold Tuesday faces the biggest risk of default, some traders say. While the Treasury puts Oct. 17 as the estimated deadline for a debt-ceiling deal, the US still has cash to pay interest on T-bills, notes and bonds through Oct. 30, many traders and economists estimate. They believe a deal will emerge to avert default but that it could come after Oct. 17. In that scenario, T-bills due Nov. 7 are the most likely to see delayed interest payment. For now, the yield on T-bills due November 7 is 0.28%, lower than the 0.43% yield on T-bills due Oct. 17. (min.zeng@wsj.com; @minzengwsj)\par \par \uc2 10:26 EDT - While a merger of Jos. A Bank (JOSB) and \uc2 Men's Wearhouse\uc2  (MW) would make for a strong menswear retailer, \uc2 Stifel Nicolaus\uc2  goes on to say that deal could be too small a view. It wonders what of big-and-tall player Destination XL (DXLG). "We believe the combination of all three companies would lead to an even more dominant player in men's retail as it would not only offer various price points, but also every size." One-stop shopping is something many males would applaud. DXLG rises 1.4%, but it's already surged more than 50% this year and still has easily outgained JOSB and MW for 2013 despite today's rally for both. (kevin.kingsbury@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 10:16 EDT - T-bills due this month to about Nov. 14 continue to get hit, with the yield on Oct. 17 T-bills yielding above 0.4%. But maturities beyond that remain sharply lower. That's a sign investors don't want to deal with potential \b \highlight22\uc2 operational\b0 \highlight\uc2  headaches if the \uc2 US government\uc2  delays payments, but still believe any missed payments would be short-lived. Thomas Urano, money-market portfolio manager at \uc2 Sage Advisory Services\uc2 , says he still holds some T-bills due Nov. 29. "If there is sign of a further rise in T-bill yields and the selling spills over to other maturities, I would think about cashing out my T-bills due Nov. 29 and put cash in T-bills in maturities later than that," he says, though he doesn't believe the US will default. (min.zeng@wsj.com; @minzengwsj)\par \par \uc2 09:59 EDT - Russ Koesterich, BlackRock's global chief investment strategist, believes the Fed under Yellen's leadership likely means no rate hike before 2015. He says implications for policy outlook are twofold. While the Fed could still taper QE3 as soon as December, the pace will be slow and dependent on the US economy. Also, "a Yellen-run Fed would likely place significant weight on the 2nd part of the Fed's dual mandate -- full employment -- even at the cost of a temporary rise in inflation." News that President Obama will nominate Yellen is boosting shorter-dated Treasurys, where yields are sensitive to the policy-rate outlook. Two-year notes up 1/32, yielding 0.369%. (min.zeng@wsj.com; @minzengwsj)\par \par \uc2 9:54 EDT - A polarized Congress and drawn-out budget debate could eat away at investors' long-term outlook on Treasurys. \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  says that would build up a "fiscal premium" demanded on longer maturities, not necessarily reflecting serious default concerns, but fears that political standoffs in the US will happen more frequently and risk mismanaging inflation. In that vein, Goldman recommends betting on a steeper yield curve: a lower 3-year yield as growth concerns increase and higher 30-year yield on that fiscal concern. The 3- to 30-year yield gap is currently 303bps; Goldman targets 340bps. (cynthia.lin@wsj.com; @cynthialin_dj)\par \par \uc2 9:48 EDT - The vitality of the early bounce in US stocks isn't impressive, and bulls will likely need some favorable headlines to help their cause today. Dip buyers may still be committed, but each time they step in and catch a falling knife reluctance builds. Nasdaq already slumps back into negative territory after falling 2% yesterday, and in the Dow, Visa was battered yesterday and shares still can't get any traction. Naming Janet Yellen as the new Fed chair offers little for bulls, a move already priced in by now. DJIA choppy early up 11; Nasdaq down 15; S&P 500 flat. (john.shipman@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 9:48 EDT - Who says the US-government shutdown hasn't hurt the economy? Pacifico Gestao de Recursos sold its stake last week in Brazilian steelmaker Gerdau (GGB), a leading producer of long-steel products in the US, after the shutdown dampened the Rio-based hedge fund's outlook for infrastructure investment in the world's biggest economy. "We were already thinking that Gerdau shares were nearing fair price, but we had thought the US business could get better. With the \uc2 US government\uc2  the way it is, it's quite improbable that infrastructure investment becomes a priority again in the near future." (paul.kiernan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 9:40 EDT - Venezuela hasn't made needed changes yet to its \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  system because of internal government debate and policy paralysis ahead of municipal elections in December, contends Eurasia. There's a rising perception that Venezuela risks an economic and social crisis if soaring inflation and runaway depreciation on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  black market aren't addressed. Officials have promised a complementary \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  mechanism, and there had been hope they would introduce more flexibility to strict \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  controls. But an earlier devaluation likely hurt President Nicolas Maduro during his run earlier this year, and he can't risk a repeat in December, says Eurasia. (ezequiel.minaya@dowjones.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par To contact the Market Talk Editors, \par Bradley Davis, 212 416-2654, bradley.davis@dowjones.com \par Kevin Kingsbury, 212 416-2354, kevin.kingsbury@dowjones.com \par John Shipman, 212 416-2181,john.shipman@dowjones.com \par Patrick Sullivan, 212 416-2326,Patrick.sullivan@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 October 09, 2013 11:13 ET (15:13 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020131009e9a9003st\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
