\par\par \b \uc2 Colombia Peso Taking Off the Training Wheels -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,773 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 September 2013\par 14:11\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global Equities News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 8:11 EDT - \b \highlight22\uc2 Colombia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  announced Friday a lighter \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, whereby starting tomorrow it'll buy a maximum $1B in 4Q to prevent peso strength vs USD. That would average about $15M daily compared with $30M so far in 2013. Keep in mind, however, there's no daily minimum or maximum, so traders think the bank will continue its heavy dollar-buying for a while on the assumption that once the Fed begins to taper, investors will reduce emerging markets bet and COP will weaken naturally, making \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  less necessary. COP closed Fri at 1,909/USD, 7.4% weaker this year. (dan.molinski@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 Call us at (212) 416-2181 or john.shipman@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 "Malaysian Shares End Down 0.4%; 1750 Floor Holds," at 1006 GMT, misstated the percentage change in the closing price of \uc2 Hong Leong Financial Group\uc2 's shares. The shares ended 1.5% lower at MYR14.52, not 15%.\par \par \uc2 1103 GMT xxxx GMT DJ: EUR/PLN is stable this morning, with some volatility expected closer to the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's euro fixing. Today's fixing will determine how much Polish farmers will receive in subsidies from EU funds: some market players expect the government may step in to strengthen the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , one dealer said. EUR/PLN currently trades at 4.2160, off about 0.2% from Friday's levels.(patryk.wasilewski@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1059 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Euro Overnight Index Average fixed slightly higher Friday at 0.085% versus Thursday's 0.082%. Monday, the interbank overnight rate is trading higher - between positive 0.13% and 0.18% versus negative 0.02% and positive 0.13% Friday. A trader says this is the typical surge often seen at the end of a month, noting rates peaked above 0.20%. The political crisis in Italy plays only a marginal role but is being watched closely by market participants, he says. The ECB may conduct additional liquidity \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  if the Italian government collapses, according to the trader. Earlier Monday, 3-month Euribor fixed at 0.225% versus 0.224% Friday. (manuel.priego-thimmel@wsj.com) Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 Markettalk.eu@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 1054 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] The lower end of the recent $1.3462/$1.3569 trading range in EUR/USD is under threat during the current session, and a downside break is anticipated. Last week's $1.3462 low survived a test during today's Asian session, but the sharp setback from Friday's $1.3565 high suggests that a deeper corrective setback - consolidating the $1.3104/$1.3569 advance - is required. Loss of $1.3462 would trigger weakness to the $1.3384 support area, and potentially $1.3355, where a measured downside objective would be generated on a break below $1.3462. Strong backup support lies at $1.3345. It would take a recovery above $1.3515 to question the EUR bearish outlook, opening the $1.3565/69 highs. EUR/USD is currently at $1.3499. -By Francis Bray, \uc2 Dow Jones Chief Technical\uc2  Analyst For Europe.(francis.bray@dowjones.com) (This article is general financial information, not personalized investment advice, as it does not consider the unique circumstances affecting an individual reader's decision to buy or sell a specific security or \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \uc2 Dow Jones\uc2  does not warrant the accuracy, completeness or timeliness of the information in this article, and any errors will not be made the basis for any claim against \uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 . The author does not invest in the instruments or markets cited in this article.)\par \par \uc2 1046 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Investors will soon focus on the fundamental outlook for EUR/GBP, which points toward further downside for the pair, says \uc2 Citigroup\uc2 . Citi economists expect Tuesday's U.K. PMIs to stay close to their recent highs, corroborating the view that the upswing of the U.K. economy is intact. "Uncertainty about the outcome of the non-confidence vote in Rome on Wednesday could keep peripheral bond markets and EUR under pressure as well. All that could continue to weigh on EUR/GBP in the near term," says Citi analyst Valentin Marinov. EUR/GBP now at 0.8355. (chiara.albanese@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1037 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] \uc2 JP Morgan\uc2  (JPM) is recommending a neutral stance on 10-year German Bunds, due to the sentiment risk posed by the debt ceiling debate in the U.S. "Although we do not expect lasting consequences from the current stalemate, uncertainty might linger for a few more weeks. The ECB meeting next week is another wild card...(and) the chatter about the possibility of another LTRO \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  has increased." However, JPM keeps its 2.10% year-end target for 10-year Bund yields (currently 1.715%). nick.cawley@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 1034 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] The strong start to the week in the December gilt future has seen the contract come within striking distance of the 1.618 Fibonacci target at 110.89. But there are warning signs that the sub-dividing bull wave from the Sept. 18 higher low at 107.22 is becoming exhaustive, as the push above 109.10 has been accompanied by dwindling volumes. The 110.89 target is the uppermost objective that the broader bull wave from the Sept. 11 contract low at 106.87 has generated, increasing the chances of a downward consolidation phase. Closing the upside gap at 110.46 would expose support at 110.12 and then 109.98. The December gilt future is at 110.64. -By Francis Bray, \uc2 Dow Jones Chief Technical\uc2  Analyst For Europe.(francis.bray@dowjones.com) (This article is general financial information, not personalized investment advice, as it does not consider the unique circumstances affecting an individual reader's decision to buy or sell a specific security or \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \uc2 Dow Jones\uc2  does not warrant the accuracy, completeness or timeliness of the information in this article, and any errors will not be made the basis for any claim against \uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 . The author does not invest in the instruments or markets cited in this article.)\par \par \uc2 1024 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] The eight-month high at $1.6180 set during the Asian hours of Monday's current session lies very close to a 29-month resistance line at $1.6203. The powerful close to the third quarter enhances the prospect for further GBP gains towards a $1.6180 Fibonacci extension target at $1.6420, and a double-bottom formation at the March/July lows at $1.4832/$1.4813 reaction lows indicates broader-term risk to $1.6672 during the fourth quarter. Support at $1.6073/80 cushions corrective weakness, and only a move below $1.6038 would uncover the $1.60 level. GBP/USD is presently at $1.6156. -By Francis Bray, \uc2 Dow Jones Chief Technical\uc2  Analyst For Europe.(francis.bray@dowjones.com) (This article is general financial information, not personalized investment advice, as it does not consider the unique circumstances affecting an individual reader's decision to buy or sell a specific security or \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \uc2 Dow Jones\uc2  does not warrant the accuracy, completeness or timeliness of the information in this article, and any errors will not be made the basis for any claim against \uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 . The author does not invest in the instruments or markets cited in this article.)\par \par \uc2 1013 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] PREVIEW: Thailand's September CPI likely picked up, rising 1.63% on year and 0.37% on month, due to higher transportation costs, according to the median forecast of seven economists in a Wall Street Journal poll. In August, the CPI rose 1.59% on year and fell 0.01% on month. September core CPI -- which excludes volatile energy and food costs -- likely rose 0.73% on year and 0.20% on month, compared with August's 0.75% on-year and 0.07% on-month increases. "The on-month rise in the headline index is mainly due to the increase in transportation costs, such as expressway fares and electricity and energy prices. However, global oil prices have been relatively stable, keeping overall energy costs unchanged locally," says \uc2 Kasikornbank\uc2 's head of capital markets research Kobsidthi Silpachai. "Despite the rising trend in consumer prices, we aren't seeing strong local demand. Therefore, the pressure shouldn't be significant and inflation should remain in control," says Thanomsri Fongarunrung, economist at \uc2 Phatra Securities\uc2 . September CPI will be released 0700 GMT Tuesday. (nopparat.chaichalearmmongkol@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1006 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Malaysian shares end lower in line with regional markets as investors worried over a possible \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  shutdown sell emerging-market assets. The benchmark KLCI closes 0.4% lower at 1768.62 in heavy trade. "The mood remains gloomy, with the market poised for another sell-off tomorrow, and all eyes will be on the U.S. budget," says a local dealer, who tips immediate support at 1750. In the broader market, gainers trail laggards 217 to 548, while 257 shares remain unchanged. Among the index stocks, index heavyweight \uc2 CIMB Group Holdings\uc2  (1023.KU) sheds 1.6% to MYR7.52 and \uc2 Hong Leong Financial Group\uc2  (1082.KU) falls 1.5% to MYR14.52, while \uc2 Petronas Chemicals\uc2  (5183.KU) gains 0.7% to MYR6.85. (abhrajit.gangopadhyay@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Corrections & Amplifications\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 This item was corrected at 1121 GMT because the original misstated the percentage change in the closing price of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Hong Leong Financial Group\f28 \uc2 \f2 's shares. The shares ended 1.5% lower at MYR14.52, not 15%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0959 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ]--\f28 \uc2 \f2 Deutsche Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  sees market reaction to Italy's latest political crisis to be fairly contained. "With the OMT (the ECB's Outright Monetary Transactions) in place, we would not expect stress levels in the market to be worse than those following the February 2013 elections, unless (i) the PD (Democratic Party) fails to unite behind a strong candidate (in a future election) and (ii) opinion polls show that a populist or anti-euro party clearly leads in the opinion polls," it says in a note. Following the mass resignation of ministers of the People of Freedom party from the coalition government led by the PD, Milan's FTSE MIB index is down 1.68%. The mild flight to quality this morning has seen the spread between Italy's BTP 10-year bond and the German bund edge closer to 300 basis points. (gilles.castonguay@dowjones.com) Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464Markettalk.eu@dowjones.com [ 30-09-13 1211GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020130930e99u000jx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
