\par\par \b \uc2 Colombia  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  Can Hold Steady for Months -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,447 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 September 2013\par 21:04\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global Equities News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 15:04 EDT - \uc2 Colombia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  held its main policy rate at 3.25% today, as was widely expected by analysts. Citi expects the rate to stay there for now, despite the improved economic outlook. "Expected inflation levels should allow the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep its policy rate on hold for the rest of 2013, and it will only start considering increases at some point between March and April of next year, when the recovery trend of the economy gets steeper." Citi adds that the bank's decision to continue its dollar purchases up to $1B total between October and December "implies some flexibility and lower average daily \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 " of $16.6M per day of the \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  are evenly distributed in 4Q. USD/COP up 0.5% at 1911.8. (erin.mccarthy@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 Call us at (212) 416-2181 or john.shipman@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 14:33 EDT - Canada is "shuffling along the slow growth path" it's been on for over a year now, as moderating gains in consumer spending, business investment, residential construction and exports undercuts economic momentum, S&P says. Moreover the economy isn't getting much uplift from fiscal policy as the government isn't longer spending as freely as in the early phase of the recovery. S&P predicts Canada's GDP growth will average 1.7% this year, 2.5% in 2014 and 2.9% in 2015. It expects labor market improvements to be slow. (nirmala.menon@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 13:53 EDT - That's \uc2 TD Securities\uc2 ' advice, saying the Fed's "obvious miscommunication" about potential QE tapering is causing investors to hit the reset button on policy expectations. Sticking to broader trends, firm says "perhaps too much transparency is a bad thing." TD doesn't see tapering starting until early 2014 now, which should only delay the dollar's rise because the overarching theme of better growth and improving fiscal balance remains intact. TD sees the greenback rising to Y100 year-end and euro falling to $1.24. It sees 10-year Treasurys yielding 2.85% by then, but not before falling toward 2.4% amid Washington's budget negotiations. (cynthia.lin@wsj.com; @cynthialin_dj)\par \par \uc2 12:33 EDT - \uc2 Standard & Poor's\uc2  says it expects the \uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2  to stay on the policy sidelines through most of 2014, and possibly into 2015. In its base-case scenario, in which it forecasts the \uc2 BOC\uc2  hiking rates in 2015, it sees the global recovery's uneven pace limiting upside potential in international trade. Without the impetus from stronger exports, gains in domestic spending in Canada will continue to be subdued as the country's consumers focus on reducing their financial leverage, S&P says. If Canada's recovery continues to build, with improving international trade and a tail wind from stronger export growth, the \uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2  could hike in 4Q 2014, it says. (don.curren@wsj.com @dbcurren)\par \par \uc2 12:10 EDT - Gains in emerging-market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  after the Fed decided not to reduce QE last Wednesday have been mostly erased. \uc2 Brown Brothers Harriman\uc2  analysts point out most EM \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are on their way to resting August-September lows. "This fits in with our view that the FOMC decision to delay tapering will continue to weigh on EM \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," they say. For instance, USD/BRL, USD/TRY, USD/ZAR, and USD/MXN have all fully given back their post-FOMC drop. In Europe, HUF and PLN have also reversed all their post-FOMC gains against the euro. Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  have held up a tad better. Investors are acknowledging that despite the delay, tapering remains on the horizon. (erin.mccarthy@wsj.com; @eemccarthy)\par \par \uc2 12:00 EDT - BofA/Merrill looks for September nonfarm payrolls to rise 170k, with unemployment rate holding steady at 7.3% in next Friday's report. "While jobless claims have fallen to levels not seen since before the labor market began contracting, we suspect that these figures overstate the likely pace of job growth: claims reflect a welcome drop in firing, but the pace of hiring remains muted, in contrast with past cycles," firm says. Strong Aug auto sales and industrial production data may mean more Sept manufacturing jobs, while disappointing back-to-school sales and weaker-than expected retail sales "likely will dent the strong hiring trend in the retail sector over the prior three months," BofA adds. (john.shipman@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 Call us at (212) 416-2181 or john.shipman@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 11:52 EDT - According to the Treasury, Congress has until October 17 to work out a higher debt limit for the country. After that, the US will run on whatever cash it has on hand, without the ability to raise additional debt. If we get to that point, it could interfere with the Treasury's debt auction schedule. For now, the Treasury has plans to announce on Oct. 17 the amount of 13- and 26-week T-bills it aims to sell on Oct. 21. It's also scheduled to announce how much 30-year TIPS it aims to sell on Oct. 24. Further delays would risk postponing a set of coupon sales set for Oct. 28-30. (cynthia.lin@wsj.com; @cynthialin_dj)\par \par \uc2 Call us at (212) 416-2181 or john.shipman@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 11:37 EDT - The stark contrast between the job creation numbers from Canada's two labor market reports for July--employer survey says gain of 60K, labor survey say 39K loss--has rekindled the debate about where the truth lies. Labor force survey comes first and is the report the market watches and reacts to. But many economists find the labor survey the more volatile and less reliable of the two, and that's what National Bank Financial is thinking this morning. Based on the soft economy and the employer survey's relative stability, it may be "the more coherent one," NBF says. (don.curren@wsj.com @dbcurren)\par \par \uc2 1527 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] European bond markets have been taking their cues from the U.S. on Friday, where Treasuries are getting a boost from uncertainty surrounding ongoing budget talks. That's helped 10-year German Bund yields (for example) to make a fresh six-week low, at 1.73%. December Bund futures are up 0.58 at 140.45. Gilts have underperformed slightly after BOE Governor \uc2 Mark Carney\uc2  said he currently sees no need for more QE, but 10-year cash gilt yields nevertheless have fallen to a new four-week low of 2.71% (2.25% 2023). (tommy.stubbington@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 11:11 EDT - Some historical context from Janney fixed-income strategist Guy LeBas, who notes Tuesday could mark the 18th funding shut down of the federal government and first since 1996. Shutdown "poses economic risks, but those risks should be largely manageable," he writes, though "a sustained government shutdown in 4Q is worth somewhere in the neighborhood of 0.9 - 1.5% of GDP (back of the envelope math), which would be a major drag on growth considering the current run rate." Bigger problem is the renewed debt ceiling tussle, but so far "it doesn't appear we've priced in much risk for the debt ceiling this time around," LeBas says. "That speaks towards a potential for further risk-off trading" leading up to the Oct 17 deadline. (john.shipman@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 Call us at (212) 416-2181 or john.shipman@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 11:07 EDT - Analysts all week have said \uc2 Colombia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  board will probably extend its year-old dollar-buying program today to keep the brakes on peso strength. That's largely because the Fed backed off on tapering its bond-buying program, which helped the peso notch strong gains last week. But this week COP is 1.2% lower, trading as weak as 1,915 per dollar this morning. Might the bank try life without \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ? After all, \b \highlight22\uc2 Colombia\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Santos favors an end to the dollar-buying. The bank board just began its meeting in downtown Bogota, and a press conference follows, usually early afternoon. (dan.molinski@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 10:03 EDT - The weak consumer sentiment report boosts fortune of bond bulls. Treasury prices rise to session high after UMich/Reuters's gauge of consumer confidence trailed economists forecasts. The data add to the risk off mood in markets already weary about the fiscal impasse. The 10-year note is 6/32 higher, yielding 2.619%. (min.zeng@wsj.com; @minzengwsj) [ 27-09-13 1904GMT ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020130927e99r000m8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
