\par\par \b \uc2 Will Colombia Keep Buying Dollars Even as Peso Drops? -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,732 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 September 2013\par 17:07\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global Equities News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 11:07 EDT - Analysts all week have said \uc2 Colombia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  board will probably extend its year-old dollar-buying program today to keep the brakes on peso strength. That's largely because the Fed backed off on tapering its bond-buying program, which helped the peso notch strong gains last week. But this week COP is 1.2% lower, trading as weak as 1,915 per dollar this morning. Might the bank try life without \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ? After all, \b \highlight22\uc2 Colombia\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Santos favors an end to the dollar-buying. The bank board just began its meeting in downtown Bogota, and a press conference follows, usually early afternoon. (dan.molinski@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 10:03 EDT - The weak consumer sentiment report boosts fortune of bond bulls. Treasury prices rise to session high after UMich/Reuters's gauge of consumer confidence trailed economists forecasts. The data add to the risk off mood in markets already weary about the fiscal impasse. The 10-year note is 6/32 higher, yielding 2.619%. (min.zeng@wsj.com; @minzengwsj)\par \par \uc2 9:37 EDT - Which direction Colombia's economy is headed is anyone's guess, and that's why most analysts figure the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  today will keep rates steady for a sixth straight month at a near-record low of 3.25%. At the Aug meeting, two board members voted for a rate reduction amid a shrinking industrial sector, a wider current account deficit and massive labor protests. But last week the government reported a much-better-than-expected 2Q GDP growth rate of 4.2% on-year vs 2.7% in 1Q, and some optimistic officials insist the next move should be rate hike. Then again, 3Q growth forecasts are weak. (dan.molinski@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 Call us at (212) 416-2181 or john.shipman@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 9:03 EDT - \uc2 Boston Fed\uc2  chief \uc2 Eric Rosengren\uc2  stays off the topic of monetary policy in a speech today, focusing instead on money-market reform. If investors today make a run on these conservative funds, he warns that because of new legislation, the government won't be able to offer the same safety nets it did in 2008. Proposals to impose a fee on those who want to withdraw money in crises times or gates to limit redemptions work against fostering stability, Rosengren argues. These rules would actually spread anxiety, compelling investors to pull out of funds in anticipation of fees or gates even if, say, only one fund is facing problems. (cynthia.lin@wsj.com; @cynthialin_dj)\par \par \uc2 Call us at (212) 416-2181 or john.shipman@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 8:58 EDT - Dollar traders are having trouble picking up any signals from the noisy US data. Consumer spending is improving, and manufacturing is picking back up; but inflation remains subdued, and jobs progress is slow. What does any of this mean for the Fed? Uncertainty in US fiscal and monetary policy is sending traders out of the dollar for now and into major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  with central banks that have indicated clearer intentions, like the New Zealand dollar or yen. Concerns are also mounting about whether Congress can cobble together a last-minute deal to avoid a government shutdown, another drag on the dollar. USDJPY at 98.46, down 0.6%; EURUSD up 0.4% at $1.3543. (nicole.hong@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 Call us at (212) 416-2181 or john.shipman@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 8:40 EDT - The bond market shrugs off the latest round of US data. Consumer spending gained some traction though matched economists forecasts. A highlight is the core PCE, the Fed's favored gauge on inflation, which continues to show tame price pressure. On an annualized basis, the reading is 1.2%, way below the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's 2% target. Contained inflation and moderate pace of growth have been the key reason why the Fed refrained from tapering this month. Fed has said there is no preset course for tapering. For now, the soothing message has broken the rising yield momentum in bonds. The 10-year note is 3/32 higher, yielding 2.63%. (min.zeng@wsj.com; @minzengwsj)\par \par \uc2 8:37 EDT - August consumer income and spending gains come in as expected, and the upward lift to July purchases mean 3Q consumer spending is adding to GDP growth. Even so, expectations for GDP are below 2% generally--economy needs all the help it can get. (kathleen.madigan@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 8:23 EDT - Bond prices perk up again, with 10-year yield near a six-week low, as investors take advantage of yesterday's selling to buy. Worries over U.S. budget talks continue to drive investors to shed riskier securities. The \uc2 US government\uc2  faces shutdown Tuesday without a deal by Monday and a bigger battle on debt ceiling looms next month. Bond markets have stabilized after the Fed's no-taper decision last week and \uc2 Chicago Fed\uc2 's Evans, the famed dove, today signals that there could be no taper this year. Personal income and spending data are due at 8:30am EDT followed by consumer sentiment survey at 9:55am EDT. 10-year note is 2/32 higher, yielding 2.634%. (min.zeng@wsj.com; @minzengwsj)\par \par \uc2 1203 GMT EUR/USD inches higher, hitting a day's high of 1.3543, after Chicago Federal Reserve President \uc2 Charles Evans\uc2  said that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may not start to reduce its monthly purchases in October. Traditionally on the dovish side, Mr. Evans said "tapering could happen October, December or January" fuelling markets' uncertainty over the Fed's future policy. The dollar weakened significantly against the dollar on these comments, with EUR/USD settling back around 1.3535 shortly after. (chiara.albanese@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1137 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] This week's sharp decline to 25.578 in EUR/CZK has prompted consolidation that has corrective upside risk to 25.825. But Thursday's volatile action sent the cross to a three-month low at 25.578, and the weekly chart below (please cut and paste into browser) shows a pennant pattern close to completion, after breaching the pattern's support line at 25.673. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.dowjoneswebservices.com/chart/view/6459"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.dowjoneswebservices.com/chart/view/6459}}}\uc2  Projecting lower from this week's lower high at 25.989, the broader bear wave from the June 27 reaction high at 26.141 has a minimum downside requirement target to meet at 25.460, coinciding with the base of the pennant pattern at 25.474. Recapturing ground above 25.825 would cast doubt over Thursday's bearish implications, and EUR bulls would be cheering if the 25.884 intra-wave lower high is broken. EUR/CZK is at 25.659. -By Francis Bray, \uc2 Dow Jones Chief Technical\uc2  Analyst For Europe.(francis.bray@dowjones.com) (This article is general financial information, not personalized investment advice, as it does not consider the unique circumstances affecting an individual reader's decision to buy or sell a specific security or \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \uc2 Dow Jones\uc2  does not warrant the accuracy, completeness or timeliness of the information in this article, and any errors will not be made the basis for any claim against \uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 . The author does not invest in the instruments or markets cited in this article.)\par \par \uc2 1120 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] A powerful downtrend is sending USD/CHF spot sustainably into new seven-month lows, and the 2013 low at $0.9023 is now within reach. The push below $0.9077 during Friday's current session is looking to break the shackles of a 19-month rising support line, and the sub-dividing structure of the bear wave from the May 22 peak at $0.9839 generates downside risk beyond $0.90 to the $0.8850/67 area on concerted USD weakness. The EUR/CHF cross has also breached a significant support line at 1.2250, signalling room for EUR weakness as far as the June 24 reaction low at 1.2215. The completed six-week EUR bear pennant continuation pattern on the daily chart indicates broader-term downside risk to 1.2125. USD/CHF and EUR/CHF are at $0.9061 and 1.2247 respectively. -By Francis Bray, \uc2 Dow Jones Chief Technical\uc2  Analyst For Europe.(francis.bray@dowjones.com) (This article is general financial information, not personalized investment advice, as it does not consider the unique circumstances affecting an individual reader's decision to buy or sell a specific security or \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \uc2 Dow Jones\uc2  does not warrant the accuracy, completeness or timeliness of the information in this article, and any errors will not be made the basis for any claim against \uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 . The author does not invest in the instruments or markets cited in this article.)\par \par \uc2 1024 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] December Bund bulls are regaining a foothold above the 140 level, having negated Thursday's bearish candle on a break above 140.09. New six-week highs are being etched, extending the powerful bull wave from the Sept. 6 contract low at 136.42 that is structured to meet a minimum upside requirement target at 141.10, projected from Monday's higher low at 138.02. The only major obstacle barring the way to 141.10 comes from the monthly Market Profile chart, highlighting resistance at 140.83. Weakness will attract support while above 139.98, and it would take a break below 139.80 to create additional downside risk to 139.49. The December Bund future is at 140.27. Market Profile is an intraday charting technique, seeking to evaluate market value as it develops during the day. The technique displays price on a vertical axis, and time/activity on a horizontal axis, creating bell curves that show normal and variable distributions in financial markets. -By Francis Bray, \uc2 Dow Jones Chief Technical\uc2  Analyst For Europe.(francis.bray@dowjones.com) (This article is general financial information, not personalized investment advice, as it does not consider the unique circumstances affecting an individual reader's decision to buy or sell a specific security or \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \uc2 Dow Jones\uc2  does not warrant the accuracy, completeness or timeliness of the information in this article, and any errors will not be made the basis for any claim against \uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 . The author does not invest in the instruments or markets cited in this article.) [ 27-09-13 1507GMT ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020130927e99r000jb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
