\par\par \b \uc2 *DJ \uc2 Colombia  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  Holds Rates for Sixth Month at 3.25%\b0\par\par\uc2 573 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 September 2013\par 19:40\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2013, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 27 Sep 2013 13:45 EDT *DJ  \b \highlight22\uc2 Colombia Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to Continue \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  Market\par \par \uc2 27 Sep 2013 13:48 EDT *DJ  \uc2 Colombia \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to Buy 'Up to' $1 Billion for Reserves Through December\par \par \uc2 27 Sep 2013 13:48 EDT *DJ  \b \highlight22\uc2 Colombia Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Puts Ceiling on \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex Intervention\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 27 Sep 2013 14:49 EDT DJ  \b \highlight22\uc2 Colombia Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Holds Rates, Tweaks \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex Intervention\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 By Dan Molinski\par \par \uc2 BOGOTA--\b \highlight22\uc2 Colombia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  left its benchmark interest rate unchanged Friday for a sixth straight month following a better-than-forecast report on economic growth, and placed a ceiling on how much it would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in light of recent weakness in the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "The economic growth report from the second quarter removed any and all doubts about the path of the economy," said Mauricio Cardenas, Colombia's Finance Minister and a voting member of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  board. "The economy is clearly recovering."\par \par \uc2 Mr. Cardenas told a press conference he and the board's other six members were unanimous in their decision to keep its main lending rate at a near-record low of 3.25%. Such an outcome was expected in a survey of private economists conducted Thursday by Dow Jones Newswires.\par \par \uc2 The bank's decision comes after two board members last month wanted to cut interest rates, arguing Colombia's shrinking industrial sector and other struggling areas of the economy needed further monetary stimulus. But last week a government report silenced calls for rate cuts as it showed the economy grew a much-larger-than-expected 4.2% in the second quarter, on-year, far surpassing the 2.7% growth rate in the first quarter.\par \par \uc2 That report has sparked optimism among many analysts that Colombia's economy, which has been hurt by broadly lower global prices for many of the commodities Colombia produces, is bouncing back. Still, some analysts fear massive labor protests throughout the nation over the past couple months might stunt third-quarter growth rates.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the bank also said it would continue a dollar-buying program aimed at preventing strength in the peso's value against the dollar, but said that it was adjusting how it \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  given recent peso weakness that renders heavy \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  unnecessary.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Cardenas announced Friday that the new policy is to only buy "up to" $1 billion total between October and December, compared to the policy of buying around $30 million daily in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market so far this year.\par \par \uc2 The peso is 8% weaker this year at around COP1,915 for a dollar, after gaining 10% in 2012. The peso's fall in recent months has largely been due to announced plans by the Federal Reserve to soon start reducing the amount of government bonds it buys. The expectations have caused many investors to leave emerging markets such as Colombia for higher or less-risky yields in the U.S. and elsewhere.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief Jose Dario Uribe said that despite the weakening trend already seen this year, the bank still would like to see it weaken further to help exporters who had a tough time selling their goods in global markets last year due to the strong local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Write to Dan Molinski at dan.molinski@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 September 27, 2013 14:49 ET (18:49 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020130927e99r003vk\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw427\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6405\pichgoal600
