\par\par \b \uc2 Will Colombia Keep Buying Dollars Even as Peso Drops? -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,356 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 September 2013\par 17:07\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 11:07 EDT - Analysts all week have said \uc2 Colombia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  board will probably extend its year-old dollar-buying program today to keep the brakes on peso strength. That's largely because the Fed backed off on tapering its bond-buying program, which helped the peso notch strong gains last week. But this week COP is 1.2% lower, trading as weak as 1,915 per dollar this morning. Might the bank try life without \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ? After all, \b \highlight22\uc2 Colombia\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Santos favors an end to the dollar-buying. The bank board just began its meeting in downtown Bogota, and a press conference follows, usually early afternoon. (dan.molinski@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 11:00 EDT - The \uc2 JC Penney\uc2  (JCP) stock offering moves \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2 ' (GS) US equity capital markets business up three spots in the 3Q market-share rankings, according to Dealogic. That sent GS to the No. 2 spot, having managed $6.36B in such offerings during the period, following \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  (JPM) with $7.41B. At the end of the day, bankers are paid to generate fees for their firm, but league-table rankings -- a source of marketing fodder and bragging rights -- offer another incentive to aggressively pursue deals. (matthew.jarzemsky@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 10:21 EDT - With the tone more negative in US markets lately, the generally bullish \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  strategist Thomas Lee offers a few reasons he sees S&P 500 "reaching at least 1775" by year end. As his "foremost" rationale, he cites S&P 500's recent all-time high, reasoning that the three prior times the index "sustainably surpassed" multi-decade highs (1958, 1982 & 1991), stocks "continued to advance for 5-15 years." One could argue, of course, that the fiscal and monetary backdrops are vastly different from those prior periods, and Fed QE has been key this time in hoisting stocks to record highs, but Lee sticks with his historical template to predict future gains. He says it's consistent with his view we're in a secular bull market. S&P 500 down 9. (john.shipman@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 10:20 EDT - Bad news for buyers in \uc2 J.C. Penney\uc2 's (JCP) 84M-share offering: the company's stock quickly broke below the $9.65 sale price this morning, recently down 9.8% at $9.40. That means anyone who bought in the offering hoping to flip the shares for a quick profit would be hard-pressed to do so. That said, some 51M JCP shares changed hands in premarket trading, a good deal of those above $9.65, according to FactSet data. Overall, JCP volume already three-and-a-half times the daily average. (matthew.jarzemsky@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 10:14 EDT - As the Fed nears winding down its stimulus measures, copper traders face something of a conundrum. Good economic data, like stronger manufacturing activity and higher employment, augur more demand for the industrial metal. But the better data could also prompt the Fed to act sooner and remove measures that kept borrowing costs low and helped spur the economic rebound in the first place. This leaves some market watchers puzzled as to the way forward for copper. "A good economy means a reduction in Fed stimulus which is bad, but surely a good economy is good?," \uc2 RBC Capital Markets\uc2  says in a note. Comex Dec copper up 1.80c, or 0.5%, at $3.3250/lb. (tatyana.shumsky@wsj.com @MissShumsky)\par \par \uc2 10:12 EDT - Vanguard Group plans to launch a global low-volatility fund, possibly in December. Vanguard Global Minimum Volatility Fund will use quantitative models to evaluate securities in the FTSE Global All-Cap Index (USD Hedged) to construct a portfolio with low volatility relative to the global equity market, according to a filing. It will seek to hedge most of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  exposure back to the U.S. dollar to reduce volatility. At a cost of 0.20% for Admiral shares and 0.30% for Investor shares, the fund "stakes new ground in the low-volatility marketplace," says \uc2 Daniel Wiener\uc2 , who tracks Vanguard funds. \{daisy.maxey@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 10:03 EDT - The weak consumer sentiment report boosts fortune of bond bulls. Treasury prices rise to session high after UMich/Reuters's gauge of consumer confidence trailed economists forecasts. The data add to the risk off mood in markets already weary about the fiscal impasse. The 10-year note is 6/32 higher, yielding 2.619%. (min.zeng@wsj.com; @minzengwsj)\par \par \uc2 9:53 EDT - Amid dismal 2Q results, \uc2 BlackBerry\uc2  (BBRY) touts its cash balance of $2.6B as a positive. Maybe so, but that's $500M less than at the end of the previous quarter. Not exactly encouraging with company's losses mounting and plans to lay off 40% of its workforce. BBRY up 1% at $8.03. (ben.dummett@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 9:42 EDT - Just because \uc2 JC Penney\uc2 's (JCP) stock has gotten so cheap doesn't make it more attractive, says IDEAglobal's chief technician Terence Gabriel, who believes the stock will keep falling, ultimately breaking below its December 2000 low at $8.63. "From a purely technical standpoint, there is no manifestation of strength building internally" that would suggest it's worth taking a shot at buying the dip, Gabriel says. "There's nothing there that says to me the stock has bottomed," he adds. "Without any indication of a change in that trend, it's a highly risky situation." JCP down 10% at $9.38; it has lost 33% the past three weeks and 52% this year. (tomi.kilgore@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 9:37 EDT - Which direction Colombia's economy is headed is anyone's guess, and that's why most analysts figure the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  today will keep rates steady for a sixth straight month at a near-record low of 3.25%. At the Aug meeting, two board members voted for a rate reduction amid a shrinking industrial sector, a wider current account deficit and massive labor protests. But last week the government reported a much-better-than-expected 2Q GDP growth rate of 4.2% on-year vs 2.7% in 1Q, and some optimistic officials insist the next move should be rate hike. Then again, 3Q growth forecasts are weak. (dan.molinski@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 9:06 EDT - The disconnect between Canada's two sets of job data is chronic, but it's particularly for the month of July. The payrolls survey released this morning showed a gain of 60,300 jobs in July after a drop in employment in June. The labor force survey for July, released back in August, showed a drop of 39,000. Economists typically advise looking at both reports on a trend basis to get a better fix on what's actually happening. This morning, CIBC World Markets says that, given the more reliable sample and reduced volatility in the employer survey, "odds are that July was indeed a robust month for hiring." (don.curren@wsj.com @dbcurren)\par \par \uc2 9:03 EDT - \uc2 Boston Fed\uc2  chief \uc2 Eric Rosengren\uc2  stays off the topic of monetary policy in a speech today, focusing instead on money-market reform. If investors today make a run on these conservative funds, he warns that because of new legislation, the government won't be able to offer the same safety nets it did in 2008. Proposals to impose a fee on those who want to withdraw money in crises times or gates to limit redemptions work against fostering stability, Rosengren argues. These rules would actually spread anxiety, compelling investors to pull out of funds in anticipation of fees or gates even if, say, only one fund is facing problems. (cynthia.lin@wsj.com; @cynthialin_dj)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par To contact the Market Talk Editors, 