\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Colombia \highlight\uc2 's Cardenas supports extended cenbank  \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 By Carlos Vargas   \par \uc2 468 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 August 2013\par 20:24\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 BOGOTA, Aug 20 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Colombia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Finance Minister \uc2 Mauricio Cardenas\uc2  said on Tuesday that under current economic conditions he would like to see the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  maintain efforts to stem gains in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and extend its dollar purchasing program beyond September.\par \par \uc2 Cardenas, who represents the government on the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's seven-member board, also told Reuters that the 3.25 percent benchmark interest rate is the "correct level" for current circumstances.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  has bought millions of dollars daily on the spot market for months in a bid to weaken the peso and ease problems for sectors like manufacturing, which struggles to compete with cheap imports, and exporters, which face high local costs and low dollar earnings.\par \par \uc2 Cardenas has said he wants a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at between 1,900 and 1,950 per dollar. The peso closed at 1,922 per dollar on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 "Under current conditions I think it's necessary to continue as we are, if everything continues as today it would be desirable to maintain the policy," Cardenas said of the dollar purchases which end on Sept. 30.\par \par \uc2 The peso has strengthened steadily over the last decade - with a respite in 2008 - from about 2,800 pesos per dollar at the end of 2003, prompting heavy lobbying by exporters and industrialists. Last year it gained 9 percent, but a potent dose of government and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , both verbal and actual, has brought it down more than 8 percent this year.\par \par \uc2 Gains in the peso have also been tempered by expectations that the U.S. Federal Reserve will soon pull its stimulus program, a move that may draw investment away from emerging markets like Colombia.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has balanced a monetary policy that aimed to prevent the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strengthening too much while also bolstering sluggish economic growth. Policymakers cut borrowing costs 200 basis points from July last year before holding them steady at the last four board meetings.\par \par \uc2 Economic growth slowed to 4 percent last year from 6.6 percent in 2011 and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  expects 4 percent expansion again this year. Cardenas estimates the economy will accelerate 4.5 percent in 2013.\par \par \uc2 "Under the actual conditions the interest rate is at its correct level, conditions are right to maintain rates," said Cardenas.\par \par \uc2 Investment has soared over the last decade as a military offensive against Marxist rebels and right-wing paramilitary groups improved security, making it easier and safer to do business in Colombia.\par \par \uc2 Growth began to flag last year as the side-effects from the global financial crisis hit consumer confidence and overseas sales - a double whammy for manufacturers.\par \par \uc2 COLOMBIA-ECONOMY/ | ABN | M | NAT | D | C | E | O | U | MTL | GRO | SOF | OIL | RNP | DNP | PTD   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020130820e98k000sp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
