\par\par \b \uc2 Colombia Peso Falls to 16-Month Low of 1,900 for a Dollar\b0\par\par\uc2 By Dan Molinski   \par \uc2 349 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Mai 2013\par 16:11\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global Equities News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 BOGOTA--Colombia's peso fell to 1,900 for a dollar Tuesday, its lowest level in 16 months, hurt by a broadly stronger dollar on rising hopes for a U.S. economic recovery.\par \par \uc2 In morning trading in Bogota, the peso fell to 1,901.95, down 1.3% from Friday's close prior to the three-day U.S. holiday weekend.\par \par \uc2 Colombia's peso was among the best-performing \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the world last year, rising 10% against the dollar as the local economy boomed on surging oil production and rising foreign investment that flooded the market with U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But this year the peso has seen a sharp turnaround, and is down 7.2% against the dollar, making it among the world's worst-performing \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The sudden reversal in fortunes is partly due to macroeconomic factors. As hopes build for a stronger U.S. economy, Colombia's economy has slowed down considerably on lower global prices for oil, coffee, coal and the country's other top export products. Colombia's economy grew 4% last year, down from a 6.6% expansion in 2011. This year, the economy will likely grow a bit above 4%, according to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Also hurting the peso, \b \highlight22\uc2 Colombia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market with daily dollar purchases of $30 million, aimed at mopping up excess dollars and weakening the peso further, in an effort to help local exporters. These dollar purchases are due to end Friday, but analysts say there is a good chance the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will extend the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  for more months, possibly until the end of the year.\par \par \uc2 Finance Minister \uc2 Mauricio Cardenas\uc2 , who is a member of the bank's board, reiterated last week that he wants to see the peso weaken to at least COP1,950 for a dollar, to ensure Colombian exporters are given a fair price for their products.\par \par \uc2 Write to Dan Molinski at dan.molinski@dowjones.com [ 28-05-13 1448GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020130528e95s000ke\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
