\par\par \b \uc2 DJ  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  CHAT: Colombian Pension Funds Could Weaken Peso More\b0\par\par\uc2 131 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Mai 2013\par 14:20\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2013, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 The pendulum is shifting for Colombia's once-mighty-but-now-weakening peso. COP, among the world's best performing \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 2012, fell to 1,856 for a dollar yesterday, its weakest in 16 months due to a broadly stronger dollar and $30M in daily \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Colombia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which aims to weaken COP to help exporters. Now, the government is trying to convince local pension funds to buy more dollars to weaken the peso even more. RBS says these retirement coffers will likely boost their foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  exposure to 15% from 7%. That's $5B in new dollar demand. (dan.molinski@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 May 23, 2013 08:20 ET (12:20 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020130523e95n003vx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw427\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6405\pichgoal600
