\par\par \b \uc2 REUTERS SUMMIT-Colombia cenbank's Zarate sees gigantic risk in guaranteed peso level\b0\par\par\uc2 898 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Mai 2013\par 22:14\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * For other news from Reuters Latin America Investment Summit, click on {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.reuters.com/summit/LatinAmerica13"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.reuters.com/summit/LatinAmerica13}}}\par \par \uc2 By Helen Murphy and Nelson Bocanegra\par \par \uc2 BOGOTA, May 21 (Reuters) - \uc2 Colombia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  board member Juan Pablo Zarate on Tuesday warned of the "gigantic" risks of guaranteeing a level for the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and highlighted the importance of a floating \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to cushion against volatile capital flows.\par \par \uc2 Colombia, which has a free-floating \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , has battled gains in the peso for years as exporters struggle with low dollar revenue and high peso costs and manufacturers face floods of cheap imports. The government has said it wants a peso devalued to about 1,900 per dollar from 1,847 today.\par \par \uc2 "The floating rate has been very important in preventing many risks in an environment of high and volatile capital flows," Zarate told the Reuters Latin America Investment Summit.\par \par \uc2 "The devaluation that we have today, I like, but I don't have a target."\par \par \uc2 Governments and central banks in advanced economies have resorted to unconventional policies to revive growth, cutting interest rates close to zero and injecting extra liquidity to boost their economies. That has led to a surge in capital flows into emerging economies, strengthening their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Latin American governments have tackled these inflows in a variety of ways. Brazil has applied strict controls on foreign capital inflows and its \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the local exchange market to tame the real, while Peru has opted for periodic \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -buying measures.\par \par \uc2 In Colombia, Finance Minister \uc2 Mauricio Cardenas\uc2  has rolled out a series of measures to help businesses cope with the "over-valued" \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , asking pension funds to diversify their portfolios overseas and buy as much as $5 billion in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has engaged in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Japan's recent monetary easing may encourage some countries to step up efforts in a "\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  war" to attract further investment.\par \par \uc2 "It's a big temptation that we should avoid," said Zarate, 42, a former deputy finance minister.\par \par \uc2 In the 1990's Colombia engaged in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  targeting, a policy that created an explosion in internal demand and a housing bubble, said Zarate. The peso began floating freely in 1999.\par \par \uc2 ADDITIONAL \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2 ?\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Colombia\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been the victim of its own success over the last decade, as improved security enabled foreign companies to build infrastructure and send workers to remote, once-off-limit areas.\par \par \uc2 Investment in capital markets has soared since a U.S.-backed offensive against Marxist rebels and paramilitary groups made it safer to do business in Colombia. Record foreign direct investment has flooded the economy, mostly in the oil and mining industries, and created structural flows of capital that are hard to prevent having an impact on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The peso has strengthened steadily over the last decade - with a respite in 2008 - from about 2,800 pesos per dollar at the end of 2003. Last year alone it gained 9 percent, but the potent dose of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has brought it down about 4 percent this year.\par \par \uc2 Zarate and his six colleagues on the bank's board will meet next week to decide whether to alter the benchmark lending rate from its current 3.25 percent. They also will be asked by Cardenas to extend its dollar purchase program from May 31 until the end of the year.\par \par \uc2 Cardenas, who represents the government on the board, will ask that policymakers continue to buy at least $30 million a day on the spot market in a bid to maintain pressure on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and prevent it strengthening further. The bank has bought roughly $3 billion on the spot market since February.\par \par \uc2 "I think that while we have excess installed capacity and have room to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  we should, but without targets and without allowing it to dominate monetary policy," Zarate said.\par \par \uc2 He declined to be drawn on how he felt about Cardenas' \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  recommendation, and said any change in monetary policy or interest rate movements will depend on new economic data during the month.\par \par \uc2 "The speed depends on certain phenomena; if the recovery phenomenon is abrupt then we have to react more quickly, but with the actual data available I don't see a need to change in an accelerated way and start increasing the rate 50 basis points or 100."\par \par \uc2 "As far as today's data is concerned, if a normalization process takes place and if the second half is better we will see when we need to go for a more neutral rate, but today I don't see the need for a strong adjustment."\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has cut 200 basis points from borrowing costs since July last year to help bolster economic growth.\par \par \uc2 Zarate said the biggest risk to emerging markets, including Colombia, over the medium term is developed nations like the United States shifting away from monetary easing.\par \par \uc2 Almost a quarter of those polled in a Reuters survey last month estimate the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will begin to increase interest rates before the end of the year to ward off future inflationary pressure.\par \par \uc2 Follow Reuters Summits on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  @Reuters_Summits\par \par \uc2 (For more summit stories see\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Helen Murphy; Editing by Chris Reese)\par \par \uc2 LATAM SUMMIT/COLOMBIA ZARATE (URGENT) | ABN | E | RBN | M | D | FUN   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020130524e95l00106\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
