\par\par \b \uc2 FACTBOX-Actions by Mexico's  \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange \highlight\uc2  commission\b0\par\par\uc2 397 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 April 2013\par 22:57\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 MEXICO CITY, April 8 (Reuters) - Mexico's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  commission said on Monday it will suspend a process of offering dollars to the market on days of sharp moves in the peso \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Mexico has a much more hands-off approach toward its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  than many of its peers, largely eschewing the direct market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  practiced by countries such as Brazil and \b \highlight22\uc2 Colombia\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Policy on the peso is set by the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  commission, a joint effort of the Finance Ministry, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and other state agencies.\par \par \uc2 The following is a list of some of board's key decisions in past years:\par \par \uc2 October 2008 - \uc2 Mexico's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  says it will auction $2.5 billion to stop the peso's most dramatic slide since the so-called Tequila Crisis in the mid-1990s.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which ends up selling $998 million in the market, also announces it will offer $400 million whenever the peso declines by at least 2 percent during a single session.\par \par \uc2 The amount of the daily dollar auction was trimmed to $300 million in March 2009 and to $250 million in May 2009. The auction mechanism was suspended in April 2010.\par \par \uc2 March 2009 - The \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  board says it will sell $100 million daily, irrespective of peso levels, in a new mechanism to stop the peso's decline.\par \par \uc2 April 2009 - The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  gets a swap line from the U.S. Federal Reserve to sell dollars among local financial institutions.\par \par \uc2 May 2009 - Daily dollar auction cut to $50 million.\par \par \uc2 October 2009 - Daily dollar auctions suspended.\par \par \uc2 February 2010 - The board resumes monthly dollar "put" option auctions to add to the country's federal reserves as a measure to cushion the economy from external turmoil. By using this mechanism, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  purchased $600 million between 1996 and 2001.\par \par \uc2 November 2011 - Mexico says it will halt monthly put option auctions and reinstate daily offers of $400 million whenever the peso declines by at least 2 percent against the dollar during a single session.\par \par \uc2 March 2013 - The \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  commission suspends the daily offers triggered by 2 percent falls in the peso, saying volatility in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets has decreased.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Krista Hughes; Editing by \uc2 Richard Chang\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 MEXICO-ECONOMY/PESO (FACTBOX) | ABN | D | E | RBN | M | U | NAT | C | O | MTL | GRO | SOF | OIL   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020130411e94800172\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw427\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6405\pichgoal600
