\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Colombia \highlight\uc2  Peso at 8-Week Low as  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  Increases  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 By Dan Molinski   \par \uc2 243 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Februar 2013\par 16:50\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global Equities News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 BOGOTA--\b \highlight22\uc2 Colombia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's peso was trading at its weakest level in eight weeks Wednesday as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  cranks up its dollar buying to support Colombian exporters stung by a persistently strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Late morning in Bogota, the peso was 0.3% weaker from Tuesday's close, at COP1,792.90 for a dollar.\par \par \uc2 Colombia's strong economy and heavy foreign investment pushed the peso 10% higher against the dollar in 2012, making it one of the world's best-performing \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But exporters of coffee, bananas and flowers--all large employers in Colombia--have been hurt by the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rise as their products become more expensive in U.S. and other markets.\par \par \uc2 The peso continued to gain strength in January, leading the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to announce at the end of the month that it would increase by 50% its minimum daily purchases of dollars, aimed at offsetting the peso's gains.\par \par \uc2 The bank now buys a minimum of $30 million daily rather than $20 million, and the bank's governor, Jose Dario Uribe, said last week that on some days it will buy more than the minimum, purchasing "$40 million or more." Analysts Wednesday said it's likely the bank has been buying more than the minimum over the past couple of days.\par \par \uc2 Write to Dan Molinski at dan.molinski@dowjones.com [ 06-02-13 1641GMT ]\par \par 