\par\par \b \uc2 ANALYSIS-Latin America lays out defenses in  \highlight22\uc2 currency \highlight\uc2  war\b0\par\par\uc2 1,225 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Januar 2013\par 19:19\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 * Colombia joins Peru and Costa Rica in flows defense\par \par \uc2 * Pundits predict bumper year for emerging market assets\par \par \uc2 * Brazil seen on sidelines this time as inflation flares\par \par \uc2 By Krista Hughes\par \par \uc2 MEXICO CITY, Jan 29 (Reuters) - Latin American policymakers are manning their defenses ahead of what could be a new battle in the "\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  wars" as flows of hot money put unwelcome upward pressure on their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Economists and investors predict that an easing in the euro zone's debt crisis, new economic stimulus measures in Japan and a return of risk appetite will fuel bumper investment flows into Latin America and other emerging markets in 2013.\par \par \uc2 Some policymakers are already at the barricades.\par \par \uc2 Colombia cut interest rates on Monday and said it would ramp up dollar purchases. Peru plans to pre-pay up to $1.5 billion in foreign debt this year and is \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  aggressively to curb \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  gains. And tiny Costa Rica unveiled a steep hike in taxes on foreign investments this month.\par \par \uc2 Even Mexico, which has a hands-off approach to \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , is considering an interest rate cut that some economists believe reflects as much a desire for a weaker peso as it does concerns about growth.\par \par \uc2 "Much of the region is once again dealing with the impacts of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  wars and QE in the developed world," said Standard Chartered senior strategist Bret Rosen, referring to the extraordinary monetary easing measures undertaken by central banks known as quantitative easing.\par \par \uc2 Low interest rates in developed economies encourage investors to look for yield elsewhere, pushing up the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of countries at the receiving end of their attentions. Stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  make exports less competitive, hurting economic growth.\par \par \uc2 It is a problem that has battered Brazilian manufacturers for several years. The government pushed back by slashing interest rates and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to weaken the real, introducing capital controls and offering tax breaks for several industries.\par \par \uc2 But Brazil has less room for maneuver than other leading Latin American economies this year as policymakers are concerned about inflation, which rose above an annualized rate of 6 percent this month.\par \par \uc2 Latin American finance ministers complained last month about the impact of stimulus policies in developed economies, and \uc2 Group of 20\uc2  economic policymakers are set to address the impact of monetary policy on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  valuations next month at a meeting in Russia.\par \par \uc2 Tools to combat appreciating \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  include buying dollars, as Colombia and Peru are doing; capital controls as in Costa Rica; and macroprudential measures, such as Peru's move to raise deposit requirements on dollar bank accounts.\par \par \uc2 Mark Mobius\uc2 , who helps oversee $51 billion in emerging market assets at fund manager \uc2 Franklin Templeton\uc2 , expects a huge influx of funds into emerging markets this year as investors tire of low-yielding government bonds.\par \par \uc2 "I think it's going to be enormous, terrific, hundreds of billions. Investors have been penalized with so low rates that they are not making anywhere near what they need," he said.\par \par \uc2 Global stock markets are up 4 percent already this year, and fund tracker EPFR said it noted a clear preference for emerging market equities over bonds, which attracted most money in 2012. The VIX index, a gauge of investor stress, fell this month to its lowest levels since 2007.\par \par \uc2 The Institute of International Finance banking group predicts capital flows to Latin America will jump 5.6 percent this year from 2012 to a record $321 billion.\par \par \uc2 Inflows to the region in 2012, including foreign direct investment as well as stock and bond investment, were already estimated to be about 30 percent higher than the "hot money" period immediately before the global financial crisis in 2008.\par \par \uc2 WHAT'S HOT?\par \par \uc2 Brazil and Mexico, as the region's two biggest economies, are likely to attract the bulk of inflows in 2013, although Brazil has managed to deter a lot of speculative investors with capital controls introduced during 2010-2011, when a rush of capital pushed the real up 12 percent against the dollar.\par \par \uc2 Foreign portfolio inflows into Brazilian stocks and bonds totaled $16.5 billion last year, down from $67.8 billion in 2010.\par \par \uc2 In its January fund managers' survey, \uc2 Bank of America\uc2 Merrill Lynch\uc2  said Brazil had the biggest share of "underweight" ratings in the history of the survey.\par \par \uc2 Mobius likes small to mid-cap companies in the region and firms exposed to the consumer sector, such as banks. \uc2 Citigroup\uc2 , which raised its stock ratings for Latin America to "overweight" as it cut back other emerging markets, likes industrials.\par \par \uc2 Mexico, for its part, is benefiting from optimism about tax and energy reforms being promised by its new government. That has helped propel stocks and bonds to record high levels.\par \par \uc2 Pacific Investment Management Co\uc2 , the California-based firm known as PIMCO that operates the world's largest bond fund, sees opportunities to make money if \uc2 Mexico's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  cuts interest rates later this year, pushing up the price of bonds.\par \par \uc2 Mexico's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has made a U-turn from warning of rate hikes to warning of rate cuts, prompting many to suspect it is worried about the peso. The peso hit a 10-month high the day central bankers met to discuss rates, and it only came off those highs when policymakers warned the following morning that they could cut rates. Lower rates would make the peso less attractive to foreign investors.\par \par \uc2 "It's no surprise that with the peso being weaker in the second half of (last) year that they were a little bit more hawkish, and now we have had a very nice run in the peso year-to-date perhaps they would be more dovish," said Michael Gomez, co-head of emerging markets at PIMCO.\par \par \uc2 London-based Capital Economics sees Mexico's peso as the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  with the most upside this year, due mainly to the lower risk of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by laissez-faire policymakers, in contrast to the record $13.9 billion bought by \uc2 Peru's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 2012 as the sol hit a 16-year high.\par \par \uc2 Chile is likely to join Peru and \b \highlight22\uc2 Colombia\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to check gains in the peso while shunning capital controls, said Capital's Neal Shearing, while Brazil is in the unusual position of trying to strengthen its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to fend off rising inflation. A stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  cuts the cost of imported goods.\par \par \uc2 "Inflation concerns are starting to creep back in Brazil, which now marks it out from Colombia, Chile and Peru who seem to be more worried about the strength of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and its impact on competitiveness," he said.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market in recent months to keep the real weaker than 2 per dollar, but this week rolled over \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps that mimic the sale of dollars in the futures market - a move with the opposite effect.\par \par \uc2 The real strengthened past the 2-per-dollar mark on Tuesday for the first time since July, and analysts said they believed policymakers would allow it to firm further if needed.\par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Jack Kimball in Bogota and Mitra Taj in Lima; Editing by Kieran Murray and \uc2 Leslie Adler\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 LATINAMERICA-ECONOMY/ (ANALYSIS) | C | D | E | M | O | U | MTL | GRO | SOF | OIL | G | RBN | ABX | BNX | FUN | RNA | AFA | CSA | LBY | RWSA | RWS | REULB | GNS  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020130129e91t0013i\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
