\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 2-INTERVIEW-Colombia cenbank director sees maximum 25 bps cut in 2012\b0\par\par\uc2 952 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 September 2012\par 20:02\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Dollar purchases have limited impact on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 * Santos seeks more \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to ease peso\par \par \uc2 * FARC peace "psychologically" good for investors\par \par \uc2 (Adds comment, details)\par \par \uc2 By Helen Murphy and Nelson Bocanegra\par \par \uc2 BOGOTA, Sept 7 (Reuters) - Colombia's economy is showing signs of improvement, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  board member Juan Jose Echavarria said, leading him to expect policymakers will hold the benchmark lending rate steady for the rest of the year or cut it by no more than a quarter percentage point.\par \par \uc2 Recent data suggests the economy will grow between 4 percent and 4.3 percent this year and register an expansion of as much as 4.3 percent in the third quarter, Echavarria said in an interview on Friday at the bank's headquarters in Bogota.\par \par \uc2 Giving his own opinion and not that of the board, Echavarria said that two months ago he would have been more pessimistic about growth in Latin America's fourth-biggest economy, but recent data like consumer confidence and energy consumption have changed his tone.\par \par \uc2 "Just two months ago we were on track for growth of 3.8 percent, but in the last (month) data gives room to be less pessimistic," he said.\par \par \uc2 Fellow board member Carlos Gustavo Cano, however, on Friday forecast growth of 3.8 percent for this year. He sees two rate cuts by year-end of 25 basis points each.\par \par \uc2 The board has already lowered its official estimate for 2012 gross domestic product growth to between 3 and 5 percent.\par \par \uc2 It views next year's economic outlook with more uncertainty as the global financial crisis hits the Andean nation's exports and industrial output, forecasting growth of between 2 and 5 percent.\par \par \uc2 "I think Europe remains an unknown and will continue that way in 2013," Echavarria said.\par \par \uc2 "And if Europe impacts China, that will cause a lot of trouble because some of the capital flows to Colombia have to do with oil. If China is complicated by Europe we will be complicated in Latin America and above all in Colombia."\par \par \uc2 The bank lowered its benchmark interest rate to 4.75 percent at its last monetary policy meeting in August in a bid to bolster the economy as the global slowdown crimps overseas sales and weak sentiment slows consumer spending.\par \par \uc2 FARC PEACE TALKS\par \par \uc2 Colombia has attracted record foreign investment in the last few years as a military offensive against insurgent groups like the Marxist FARC makes once off-limits mountains and plains accessible to international oil and mining companies.\par \par \uc2 The $330 billion economy could attract as much as $17 billion in foreign direct investment this year, the government has said, up from about $2 billion in 2000.\par \par \uc2 President \uc2 Juan Manuel Santos\uc2 ' bid to bring an end to five decades of war with the Revolutionary Armed Forces of Colombia, or FARC, would have a positive impact on the economy, Echavarria said, boosting growth by about two percentage points and allowing the bank to cut interest rates further.\par \par \uc2 While international companies in the booming oil and mining sector have largely ignored the rebel threat in the last several years - investing in previously no-go areas - peace with the rebels would bring a respite of attacks on wells and other economic infrastructure, helping them expand \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Echavarria said the economy would not feel the peace dividend immediately and could be affected if there is "a lot of trouble over the next year" but in the long term peace would bring "a lot of positives."\par \par \uc2 "Right now there is a lot of good news out of Colombia; the macro(economic) side has been well managed and I think psychologically, for an international investor, it will help a lot if peace is also reached," Echavarria said.\par \par \uc2 BANK \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 The flood of foreign investment into \b \highlight22\uc2 Colombia\b0 \highlight\uc2  has helped make its peso \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  one of the strongest worldwide, prompting the government to pile pressure on the bank to prevent the firming \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from damaging the economy.\par \par \uc2 A strong peso hurts exporters and also some manufacturers who earn in dollars but pay costs and wages in pesos.\par \par \uc2 Echavarria said there was a limit to what the bank could do to ease gains in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the fourth-biggest gainer worldwide this year, firming 7.1 percent so far this year.\par \par \uc2 Board members are "more or less in agreement" that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  programs, like the current dollar purchase program of at least $20 million daily, are not that effective in managing movements in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , he said.\par \par \uc2 "Almost all international and Colombian studies demonstrate that they have a limited impact," said Echavarria, one of seven policymakers on the bank's board.\par \par \uc2 Santos on Friday reiterated a call for the bank to boost the level of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.\par \par \uc2 "I think the bank could increase a little more its daily dollar purchases, it has the room and the reserves to do it," Santos told a conference of exporters in Medellin.\par \par \uc2 "The government too will continue buying dollars; we will use the excess we have in the Treasury for the purchases and we'll continue with the policy of keeping our savings overseas," said Santos, a former finance minister.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been buying as much as $20 million a day since February and last month it said it would purchase $700 million in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market from the end of August through September in an effort to stem gains by the peso.\par \par \uc2 The government has bolstered efforts of the bank, buying at least $800 million over the past few weeks.\par \par \uc2 (Editing by Gary Hill and James Dalgleish)\par \par \uc2 COLOMBIA-ECONOMY/ (UPDATE 2, INTERVIEW) | ABN | D | M | E | RBN | FUN   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020120910e8970015g\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw427\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6405\pichgoal600
